se “one = 0” (no uncovered squares) violates this requirement and therefore does **not** satisfy the constraints.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of uncovered unit squares with one uncovered cell in each row and each column is equivalent to a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the uncovered cell in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) Boolean matrix  \n\n\\[\nM_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n0 & \\text{if }j=\\sigma(i),\\\\[2mm]\n1 & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(M=J-P_{\\sigma}\\), where  \n\n* \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix \\((J_{ij}=1\\ \\forall i,j)\\),  \n* \\(P_{\\sigma}\\) is the permutation matrix of \\(\\sigma\\) \\((P_{\\sigma})_{ij}=1\\iff j=\\sigma(i)\\).\n\nA rectangular tile whose sides lie on grid lines and which does not cover any uncovered square corresponds to a pair of subsets  \n\\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the rows occupied by the tile) and  \n\\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the columns occupied by the tile) such that  \n\n\\[\nC\\cap\\sigma(R)=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe tile covers exactly the cells \\(\\{(i,j):i\\in R,\\ j\\in C\\}\\), i.e. the Boolean rank‑1 matrix \\(u_R v_C^{\\mathsf T}\\) where  \n\n\\[\n(u_R)_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 & i\\in R,\\\\\n0 & i\\notin R,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n(v_C)_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 & j\\in C,\\\\\n0 & j\\notin C .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nA family of tiles covering all cells with value \\(1\\) in \\(M\\) is precisely a Boolean decomposition  \n\n\\[\nM=\\bigvee_{t=1}^{k} u_{R_t}v_{C_t}^{\\mathsf T},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\bigvee\\) denotes entrywise logical OR.  \nHence the minimum number of tiles equals the Boolean rank of \\(M\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound – a construction with \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nR_j=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\},\\qquad \nC_j=\\