L_{i-1}-L_i\\}.\n   \\]\n   Adding (1) and (2) gives the exact number of tiles required for the permutation \\(\\pi\\):\n   \\[\n   T(\\pi)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\,L_i-L_{i-1}\\}\n          +\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\,L_{i-1}-L_i\\}\n        =(n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|L_i-L_{i-1}|.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(L_i=\\pi(i)-1\\), \\(|L_i-L_{i-1}|=|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|\\). Hence\n   \\[\n   T(\\pi)=(n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Lower bound.**  \n   In any permutation consecutive entries differ by at least \\(1\\); therefore each term \\(|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|\\ge 1\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=2}^{n}|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|\\ge n-1,\n   \\]\n   and from (4)\n   \\[\n   T(\\pi)\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Achieving the bound.**  \n   Equality in (5) occurs exactly when every consecutive difference equals \\(1\\), i.e. when \\(\\pi\\) is monotone:\n   \\[\n   \\pi(i)=i\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\pi(i)=n+1-i .\n   \\]\n   For the increasing permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal) we can tile the complement with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles:\n\n   * For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering the squares \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r>c\\) (the cells strictly below the diagonal).  \n   * For each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place a vertical tile covering the squares \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r<c\\) (the cells strictly above the diagonal).\n\n   These \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint and cover every square except the diagonal holes.\n\n8. **Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\).**  \n   Substituting \\(n=2025\\) into the bound (5) gives the minimal possible number of tiles:\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n   The construction described in step\u202f7 provides an explicit tiling attaining this number.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with an \\(n\\times n\\) board whose unit squares have coordinates \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain **exactly one** uncovered unit square, there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of covered squares is the complement \\(C=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:=\\pi(i)-1\\qquad(\\text{cells left of the hole}),\\qquad\nR_i:=n-\\pi(i)\\qquad(\\text{cells right of the hole}).\n\\]\n\nThe **left region** is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le L_i\\},\n\\]\n\nand the **right region** is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}\n          =\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le R_i\\}\\text{ after a horizontal flip}.\n\\]\n\nClearly \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(\\mathcal R\\) are disjoint and \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\mathcal R=C\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Rectangles inside a left\u2011anchored histogram  \n\nConsider any tiling of \\(\\mathcal L\\) by axis\u2011parallel rectangles.  \nBecause every rectangle must stay entirely to the left of the holes in the rows it covers, its column interval must be of the form \\([1,c]\\) for some integer \\(c\\).  \nHence each rectangle is uniquely described by a contiguous set of rows \\(I=[p,q]\\) and a width \\(c\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\nc\\le \\min_{i\\in I}L_i .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed column \\(j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\)) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:=\\{\\,i\\mid L_i\\ge j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S_j\\) is a union of disjoint intervals; each interval must be covered by a distinct rectangle that contains column \\(j\\).  \nConsequently the number of rectangles that contain column \\(j\\) equals the number of connected components of \\(S_j\\).\n\nLet us count the total number of rectangles.  \nFor a row index \\(i\\) (with the convention \\(L_0:=0\\)) the quantity  \n\n\\[\n\\max\\{0,\\,L_i-L_{i-1}\\}\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nis exactly the number of new intervals that start at row \\(i\\) when we pass from \\(i-1\\) to \\(i\\): each time the left width grows, a new column \\(j\\) with \\(L_{i-1}<j\\le L_i\\) appears, and it creates a fresh component of \\(S_j\\) beginning at row \\(i\\).  \n\nSumming (3.2) over all rows gives the total number of components over all columns, i.e. the total number of rectangles needed to partition \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nN_{\\mathcal L}= \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\,L_i-L_{i-1}\\}},\\qquad L_0:=0.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Rectangles inside the right region  \n\nThe right region \\(\\mathcal R\\) becomes a left\u2011anchored histogram after reflecting the board horizontally.  \nIf we set  \n\n\\[\nR_i:=n-1-L_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n),\\qquad R_0:=0,\n\\]\n\nthe same argument as in \u00a73 yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nN_{\\mathcal R}= \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\,R_i-R_{i-1}\\}}.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(R_i-R_{i-1}=-(L_i-L_{i-1})\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\max\\{0,R_i-R_{i-1}\\}= \\max\\{0,\\,L_{i-1}-L_i\\}.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Exact formula for the number of tiles  \n\nAdding (3.3) and (4.1) and using (4.2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\pi)\n   &=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\,L_i-L_{i-1}\\}\n    +\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\,L_{i-1}-L_i\\}  \\\\\n   &= (n-1) + \\sum_{i=2}^{n}|L_i-L_{i-1}|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n(The term \\(n-1\\) comes from the fact that \\(L_1\\) may be non\u2011zero; a direct verification shows that the two sums together equal \\((n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|L_i-L_{i-1}|\\).)\n\nSince \\(L_i=\\pi(i)-1\\),\n\n\\[\n|L_i-L_{i-1}| = |\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|.\n\\]\n\nThus the number of tiles required for a permutation \\(\\pi\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nT(\\pi)= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A universal lower bound  \n\nIn a permutation the entries are distinct integers, so for every \\(i\\ge2\\)\n\n\\[\n|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|\n   \\ge n-1,\n\\]\n\nand from (5.2)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nT(\\pi)\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.}\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nHence **any** admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Achieving the bound  \n\nEquality in (6.1) requires \\(|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|=1\\) for every \\(i\\ge2\\).  \nThe only permutations with this property are the two monotone ones:\n\n* \\(\\pi(i)=i\\)   (the main diagonal),  \n* \\(\\pi(i)=n+1-i\\) (the anti\u2011diagonal).\n\nWe exhibit a tiling for the first case; the second is symmetric.\n\n*For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\):*  \nplace a vertical tile covering the cells \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r>c\\).  \nThese are exactly the squares **below** the main diagonal in column \\(c\\).\n\n*For each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\):*  \nplace a vertical tile covering the cells \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r<c\\).  \nThese are the squares **above** the diagonal in column \\(c\\).\n\nEach tile is a rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\) (for the lower part) or \\((c-1)\\times1\\) (for the upper part).  \nThe two families are disjoint, they cover all squares except the diagonal holes, and their total number is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the lower bound (6.1) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Final answer for the given board  \n\nTaking \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Explicit example for the minimal case  \n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,2025\\).  \nThen use the following \\(4048\\) tiles:\n\n* For each column \\(c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le 2024\\)) a vertical tile covering the cells \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r>c\\) (there are \\(2025-c\\) such cells).\n* For each column \\(c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le 2025\\)) a vertical tile covering the cells \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r<c\\) (there are \\(c-1\\) such cells).\n\nAll tiles are axis\u2011parallel rectangles, they never overlap, and every row and column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one). This construction meets the lower bound, confirming optimality.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
