n's contribution: we derived N_R = sum max{0, R_i - R_{i-1}} = max{0, n-π(1)} + sum_{i=2}^n max{0, L_{i-1} - L_i}. So total T = L_1 + max{0, n-π(1)} + sum_{i=2}^n |L_i - L_{i-1}|. Since L_1 = π(1)-1, max{0, n-π(1)} = n-π(1) = n-1 - L_1. So T = L_1 + (n-1 - L_1) + sum_{i=2}^n |L_i - L_{i-1}| = (n-1) + sum_{i=2}^n |L_i - L_{i-1}|. So the (n-1) term emerges from sum of L_1 and max{0, n-π(1)}. Indeed, the sum of the first terms from left and right region yields n-1 regardless of π(1). So the formula T = (n-1) + sum_{i=2}^n |L_i - L_{i-1}| is correct after all. Let's verify: N_L = sum_{i=1}^n max{0, L_i - L_{i-1}} = max{0, L_1 - L_0} + sum_{i=2}^n max{0, L_i - L_{i-1}} = max{0, L_1} + sum_{i=2}^n max{0, L_i - L_{i-1}} = L_1 + sum_{i=2}^n max{0, L_i - L_{i-1}} (since L_1 >=0). N_R = sum_{i=1}^n max{0, R_i - R_{i-1}} = max{0, R_1 - R_0} + sum_{i=2}^n max{0, R_i - R_{i-1}} = max{0, n-π(1)} + sum_{i=2}^n max{0, L_{i-1} - L_i} = (n-π(1)) + sum_{i=2}^n max{0, L_{i-1} - L_i} (since n-π(1) >=0). So total T = L_1 + (n-π(1)) + sum_{i=2}^n (max{0, L_i - L_{i-1}} + max{0, L_{i-1} - L_i}) = L_1 + (n-π(1)) + sum_{i=2}^n |L_i - L_{i-1}|. But L_1 = π(1)-1, n-π(1) = n-1 - L_1. So L_1 + (n-π(1)) = n-1. So T = (n-1) + sum_{i=2}^n |L_i - L_{i-1}|. So indeed the formula is correct. Good.\n\nThus the lower bound: sum_{i=2}^n |π(i)-π(i-1)| >= n-1 because each difference is at least 1. So T >= (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. So lower bound holds.\n\nNow we need to verify that the construction achieving 2n-2 works for identity permutation (or anti-diagonal). The solution describes vertical tiles above and below diagonal: For each column c (1 <= c <= n-1), place a vertical tile covering rows c+1..n in column c (below diagonal). That's a rectangle of size (n-c) x 1. For each column c (2 <= c <= n), place a vertical tile covering rows 1..c-1 in column c (above diagonal). That's a rectangle of size (c-1) x 1. These tiles are disjoint and cover all squares except diagonal squares. Indeed, for column c, the tile T_c^- covers rows > c (i.e., rows c+1..n). The tile T_c^+ covers rows < c (i.e., rows 1..c-1). The diagonal square (c,c) is uncovered. So each column has exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal). Each row also has exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal). So the condition is satisfied. Number of tiles: T_c^- for c=1..n-1 gives n-1 tiles; T_c^+ for c=2..n gives n-1 tiles; total 2n-2. So construction works.\n\nThus answer 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to check reasoning validity: The solution uses valid mathematical principles: combinatorial reasoning, decomposition, lemma about minimal rectangles covering left-justified histogram, reflection, absolute differences, lower bound argument, constructive example. All are valid methods. No invalid reasoning like trial-and-error. So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation issues. We need to identify any justification gaps or critical errors.\n\nPotential issues: The solution's Lemma 1 proof is somewhat informal but seems sufficient. The derivation of formula (7) may have a small gap: they claim sum_{i=1}^n max{0, L_i - L_{i-1}} + sum_{i=1}^n max{0, L_{i-1} - L_i} = (n-1) + sum_{i=2}^n |L_i - L_{i-1}|. They might have omitted explicit justification for the (n-1) term, but they gave reasoning that L_1 + (n-π(1)) = n-1. This is a small justification gap but not a critical error. The solution does not explicitly show that L_1 + (n-π(1)) = n-1, but it's easy to see. So it's a justification gap.\n\nAlso, the solution states \"Since each absolute difference >=1 because entries are distinct integers.\" That's correct. But they might not explicitly argue that sum_{i=2}^n |π(i)-π(i-1)| >= n-1; they just state it. That's a small gap but fine.\n\nThe construction: They claim each T_c^- and T_c^+ are rectangles of size (n-c) x 1 and (c-1) x 1. That's correct. They claim they are disjoint because diagonal separates them. That's fine.\n\nPotential missing justification: They didn't explicitly argue that the vertical tiles inde