y one** interval in each of its \\(h_k\\) rows. Consequently the total number of row‑intervals covered by all rectangles equals \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{t}h_k\\).\n\nEvery non‑empty row‑segment must be covered by at least one such interval; therefore, by (1),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{t}h_k \\ge \\Sigma_{\\text{row}} \\ge 2n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Column‑segment bound\n\nThe same argument applied to columns yields the analogous decomposition\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(q(j),j)\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwith top and bottom column‑segments\n\\[\nT_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<q(j)\\},\\qquad\nB_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid q(j)<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nExactly the same reasoning gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{t}w_k \\ge 2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of rectangles\n\nAdding (2) and (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{t}(h_k+w_k)\\ge (2n-2)+(2n-2)=4n-4. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor every rectangle \\(k\\) we have \\(h_k\\ge1\\) and \\(w_k\\ge1\\), whence \\(h_k+w_k\\ge2\\). Summing this trivial inequality over all \\(t\\) rectangles gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{t}(h_k+w_k)\\ge 2t. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nCombining (4) and (5) yields\n\n\\[\n2t\\ge 4n-4\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t\\ge 2n-2. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** tiling satisfying the problem’s condition uses at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal:\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) create two rectangles:\n\n* a horizontal rectangle\n  \\[\n  H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n  which is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile;\n\n* a vertical rectangle\n  \\[\n  V_i=\\{(j,i)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n  which is an \\((n-i)\\times1\\) tile.\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{V_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, they avoid all diagonal squares, and together they cover every off‑diagonal cell. The total number of rectangles is\n\n\\[\n| \\{H_i\\}|+|\\{V_i\\}| = (