be covered by rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines and which are pairwise interior‑disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Inductive lower bound)  \n\nLet \\(T(n)\\) be the minimum possible number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board satisfying the above conditions.  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nT(1)=0,\\qquad T(n)\\ge T(n-1)+2\\quad\\text{for }n\\ge2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(n=1\\) the single square is uncovered, so no tile is needed and \\(T(1)=0\\).\n\nAssume \\(n\\ge2\\) and let \\(r\\) be the (unique) row with \\(p(r)=1\\); such a row exists because \\(p\\) is a permutation.  \n\n*Row \\(r\\).*  \nAll squares of row \\(r\\) except the hole are the squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(r,j)\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny tile that contains one of these squares cannot contain column 1, for otherwise it would also contain the hole \\((r,1)\\).  \nConsequently every tile intersecting row \\(r\\) is completely contained in the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{r\\}\\times\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince there are \\(n-1\\) squares in (1) to be covered, at least one tile is required for row \\(r\\).\n\n*Column 1.*  \nAll squares of column 1 except the hole are the squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,1)\\mid i\\neq r\\}.\n\\]\n\nA tile containing one of these squares cannot contain row \\(r\\), otherwise it would contain the hole \\((r,1)\\).  \nHence every tile intersecting column 1 is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{r\\}\\times\\{1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAgain at least one tile is needed for column 1.\n\nThe tiles described in (1) and (2) are disjoint from each other and from any tile that lies completely inside the subboard obtained by deleting row \\(r\\) and column 1.  \n\nDelete row \\(r\\) and column 1.  The remaining board is an \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) grid.  \nFor each remaining row \\(i\\neq r\\) the uncovered square is still at column \\(p(i)\\), and after the deletion of column 1 we may relabel the columns \\(2,\\dots ,n\\) as \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  Thus the uncovered squares of the subboard again form a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n\nConsequently the restriction of any tiling of the original board to the subboard yields a tiling of an \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) instance, and therefore uses at least \\(T(n-1)\\) tiles.  \n\nSumming the contributions we obtain  \n\n\\[\nT(n)\\ge T(n-1)+2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Solution of the recurrence)\n\nThe recurrence (3) with the initial value \\(T(1)=0\\) solves to  \n\n\\[\nT(n)\\ge 2n-2\\qquad (n\\ge1).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By induction on \\(n\\).  \nThe case \\(n=1\\) gives \\(T(1)=0=2\\cdot1-2\\).  \nAssume \\(T(n-1)\\ge 2(n-1)-2\\).  Using (3),\n\n\\[\nT(n)\\ge T(n-1)+2\\ge\\bigl[2(n-1)-2\\bigr]+2=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\nThus any tiling of a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board satisfying the problem’s condition uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles)\n\nLet us choose the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n*Vertical tiles (left of the diagonal).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) has size \\((n-j)\\times1\\); it does not contain any diagonal square because the diagonal square in column \\(j\\) is \\((j,j)\\), which lies above the rectangle.  \nThe rectangles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares strictly left of the diagonal.\n\n*Horizontal tiles (right of the diagonal).*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) has size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\); it avoids the diagonal because the diagonal square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,i)\\), which lies to the left of the rectangle.  \nThe rectangles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares strictly right of the diagonal.\n\nThe two families are disjoint, they cover every non‑diagonal square exactly once, and no tile contains a hole.  \nHence we obtain a tiling with exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nLemma 2 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and Lemma 3 exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\" for complete rigor. The claim is mathematically correct and the r