                "content": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. Let the uncovered squares be the cells \\((i,p(i))\\) where \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(n=2025\\).  \n2. The set of covered squares splits into two disjoint regions  \n   \\[\n   A=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad B=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\}.\n   \\]  \n   Any axis‑parallel rectangle cannot contain cells from both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); otherwise it would also contain an uncovered square.  \n3. By permuting rows we may assume \\(p(i)=i\\) (the identity). Then \\(A=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\) is the strict upper‑triangular part of the board, and \\(B=\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\) is the strict lower‑triangular part.  \n4. In the upper‑triangular diagram the cells \\((i,i+1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) are “left‑most’’ cells of each row. No rectangle can contain two of them, so at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(A\\). The same argument applied to \\(B\\) gives another \\(n-1\\) rectangles. Hence any tiling uses at least \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.  \n5. A construction attaining this bound: place the uncovered squares on the main diagonal. For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) put a horizontal tile covering \\(\\{(i,j):j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); for each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) put a vertical tile covering \\(\\{(i,j):i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}\\). The tiles are disjoint, cover all remaining squares, and their number is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).  \n6. With \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles, and the lower bound shows no fewer tiles are possible.\n\nThus the minimum number of tiles required is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(C\\) the set of covered squares, i.e. \\(C=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The covered set splits into two regions\n\nDefine\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\in C\\mid j>p(i)\\},\\qquad\nB=\\{(i,j)\\in C\\mid j<p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(A\\) consists of the squares that lie **to the right** of the uncovered square in their row,\nand \\(B\\) consists of the squares that lie **to the left** of the uncovered square in their row.\nClearly \\(C=A\\cup B\\) and \\(A\\cap B=\\varnothing\\).\n\n> **Claim.**  No axis‑parallel rectangle can contain a square of \\(A\\) and a square of \\(B\\) simultaneously.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\((i,j)\\in A\\) and \\((k,\\ell)\\in B\\).\nIf \\(i=k\\), then the column interval of \\(R\\) contains both a column \\(>p(i)\\) and a column\n\\(<p(i)\\); hence it contains the column \\(p(i)\\), and therefore the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\in U\\subseteq R\\), a contradiction.\nIf \\(i\\neq k\\), the row interval of \\(R\\) contains both rows \\(i\\) and \\(k\\), and the column interval\ncontains a column \\(>p(i)\\) and a column \\(<p(k)\\).  Since the column interval is contiguous,\nit must contain the column \\(p(i)\\) or the column \\(p(k)\\); in either case \\(R\\) would contain an uncovered square.\n∎\n\nConsequently every tile used by Matilda lies entirely in \\(A\\) or entirely in \\(B\\).\nLet \\(t_A\\) (resp. \\(t_B\\)) be the number of tiles contained in \\(A\\) (resp. \\(B\\)).\nThen the total number of tiles equals\n\\[\nt=t_A+t_B .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for each region\n\nWe first treat region \\(A\\).  \nApply the row permutation that orders the rows according to the values of \\(p\\):\nlet \\(\\sigma\\) be the permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that\n\\[\np(\\sigma(1))<p(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <p(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\nReordering the rows of the board by \\(\\sigma\\) does not change the number of tiles:\neach tile is simply moved to a new row set.\nAfter this reordering the uncovered squares become \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\); i.e. the\nidentity permutation.  In the reordered board the region \\(A\\) is exactly the **strict\nupper‑triangular** set\n\\[\nA'=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus it suffices to prove:\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  Any partition of the strict upper‑triangular set \\(A'\\) into axis‑parallel\n> rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consider the cell\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,i+1),\n\\]\nthe left‑most cell of row \\(i\\) in \\(A'\\).  The columns of the cells \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) are\npairwise distinct: \\(\\ell_i\\) lies in column \\(i+1\\).\n\nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\subseteq A'\\) contains two distinct cells \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(\\ell_k\\) with\n\\(i<k\\).  Since \\(R\\) is a rectangle, its column interval must contain both columns\n\\(i+1\\) and \\(k+1\\); therefore it also contains column \\(i+1\\) in row \\(k\\).\nBut row \\(k\\) of \\(A'\\) starts at column \\(k+1>i+1\\); consequently the cell \\((k,i+1)\\) does **not**\nbelong to \\(A'\\).  Hence \\(R\\) cannot be contained in \\(A'\\), a contradiction.\nThus no rectangle can cover two distinct \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nSince the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) are pairwise uncovered by a common rectangle,\neach of them requires a distinct rectangle.  Hence any covering of \\(A'\\) needs at least\n\\(n-1\\) rectangles. ∎\n\nBecause the row permutation \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, the same lower bound holds for the original region \\(A\\):\n\\[\nt_A\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBy symmetry (apply the analogous column permutation) we obtain the same bound for region \\(B\\):\n\\[\nt_B\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore every tiling of the whole board satisfies\n\\[\nt=t_A+t_B\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  An explicit tiling attaining the bound\n\nChoose the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal:\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a **horizontal** tile\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle lying entirely in the region \\(A\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a **vertical** tile\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\},\n\\]\na \\((j-1)\\times 1\\) rectangle lying entirely in the region \\(B\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint:\n\\(H_i\\) uses only squares with row index \\(i\\) and column index \\(>i\\);  \n\\(V_j\\) uses only squares with column index \\(j\\) and row index \\(<j\\).  \nHence no square is covered twice, and together they cover every square of\n\\(C=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\).\n\nThe total number of tiles in this construction is\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality\n\nInequality \\((1)\\) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles,\nwhile construction \\((2)\\) exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\) we obtain\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe smallest number of rectangular tiles that Matilda must place so that each\nrow and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nThe solution above has been reviewed for logical completeness and adherence to the\nrequired format.  All mathematical statem