of \\(k\\)), we obtain exactly \\(n-1\\) distinct blocks in total:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal I_L|=n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The right region\n\nDefine, for \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{\\,i\\mid p(i)<k\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nB'_k=\\text{maximal contiguous blocks of }T_k .\n\\]\n\nThe same reasoning as above, now adding rows in **increasing** order of \\(p(i)\\), shows that the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I_R=\\bigcup_{k=2}^{n}B'_k\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n-1\\) distinct blocks:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal I_R|=n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA tile lying in the right region can cover at most one block of a given column, so any tiling of \\(\\mathcal R\\) needs at least \\(|\\mathcal I_R|\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Global lower bound\n\nSince left‑tiles and right‑tiles are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal I_L|+|\\mathcal I_R|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.  Hence for every permutation \\(p\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{#tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the **identity permutation** \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*For the left region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the rectangle with rows \\(j+1,\\dots ,n\\) and the single column \\(j\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) tiles cover precisely the cells \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\}\\), which are exactly the left region.\n\n*For the right region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{R}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe rectangle with rows \\(1,\\dots ,j-1\\) and column \\(j\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) tiles cover precis