tile might have left cells? Actually if p(i') <= c, then tile's leftmost column c is >= p(i'), so tile's columns are to the right of the hole. So tile would be entirely right of hole for that row. So tile could be left of hole for some rows (if p(i) > d) and right of hole for other rows (if p(i) <= c). But does that cause tile to contain hole for any row? No, because for rows where p(i) > d, tile's columns are left of hole; for rows where p(i) <= c, tile's columns are right of hole. So tile could be \"straddling\" the holes across rows: some rows have holes left of tile, some rows have holes right of tile. But does that violate the condition that each row has exactly one uncovered square? The tile would not cover the hole for any row; it would be either left or right. So it's possible that a tile could be partially left region for some rows and partially right region for others. However, the solution's argument says tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have a permutation p(i). For each row i, the hole is at column p(i). The left region L_i = {j < p(i)}. The right region R_i = {j > p(i)}. The union of all left cells across rows is L = {(i,j) | j < p(i)}. Similarly R = {(i,j) | j > p(i)}.\n\nNow consider a tile T = [a,b] rows, [c,d] columns. For each row i in [a,b], we need that T does not contain the hole (i,p(i)). So we need p(i) not in [c,d] for any i in [a,b]. So for each i, either p(i) < c (hole left of tile) or p(i) > d (hole right of tile). So for each i, p(i) is either < c or > d. So the row set can be partitioned into those where p(i) < c (hole left) and those where p(i) > d (hole right). For rows where p(i) < c, the tile's columns are to the right of the hole, thus tile's cells are all in R_i for those rows. For rows where p(i) > d, tile's columns are left of the hole, thus tile's cells are all in L_i for those rows. So tile can contain cells from both L and R across different rows, but not both for the same row. However, the solution's claim \"tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R\" means that tile's cells are all in L or all in R across all rows. But from the above, tile could have some rows with p(i) < c (hole left) and some rows with p(i) > d (hole right). In that case, tile would contain cells that are in R for some rows and L for others. So tile would not be entirely in L or entirely in R. So the claim might be false.\n\nBut is it possible for a tile to have both types of rows? Let's examine the permutation p(i). For a tile with column interval [c,d], we need p(i) not in [c,d] for all i. So p(i) must be either < c or > d. So the set of p(i) values for rows in the tile must avoid the interval [c,d]. So the permutation values for those rows must be all less than c or all greater than d; they cannot be a mixture of both less than c and greater than d? Actually they could be a mixture: some rows have p(i) < c, some have p(i) > d. That's allowed because p(i) values are distinct across rows. So it's possible that the tile's rows include some rows whose holes are left of the tile and some rows whose holes are right of the tile. In that case, the tile's cells are in R for those rows where holes left, and in L for those rows where holes right. So tile would contain cells from both L and R.\n\nBut does the tile need to be contiguous in rows? Yes, rows are an interval [a,b]. So the rows are consecutive. So we need a consecutive set of rows where the permutation values are either all less than c or all greater than d, but they could be mixed across the interval. However, can a permutation have such a property? Let's think: Suppose we have a tile covering rows i = a,...,b. The permutation values p(i) for those rows are some set of distinct integers. For the tile to avoid [c,d], we need each p(i) either < c or > d. So the set of p(i) values for rows a..b must be a subset of {1,...,c-1} ∪ {d+1,...,n}. Could it be that some are less than c and some greater than d? Yes, possible. For example, suppose c=5, d=7, n=10. Then p(i) must be in {1,2,3,4} ∪ {8,9,10}. So rows could have holes at columns 2,9,3,10, etc. So tile would have rows with holes left of tile (p(i) < 5) and rows with holes right of tile (p(i) > 7). So tile would be partly left region for some rows and partly right region for others.\n\nThus the claim that tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R is not generally true. However, the solution's argument might rely on the fact that tile's columns are contiguous, and the holes are a permutation, but maybe they argue that if tile contains a left cell (j < p(i)), then d < p(i) for that row. Since d is same across rows, this implies for any other row i' in the tile, we must have d < p(i') as well, else tile would contain hole for that row? Wait, they argued: \"If the tile contains a cell (i,j) with j<p(i), then for the same row i the hole lies at column p(i). Since the tile's column interval is the same for all rows it covers, we must have d<p(i); otherwise the tile would also contain the hole (i,p(i)). Thus the whole tile is contained in L.\" Let's examine: If tile contains a left cell for row i, then indeed d < p(i). Since d is same for all rows, we have d < p(i) for that row. But does that guarantee that for any other row i' in the tile, we also have d < p(i')? Not necessarily; we only know that d < p(i) for that particular i. For another row i', p(i') could be less than or equal to d, but then tile would contain the hole (i', p(i')). But does that happen? If p(i') <= d, then tile's column interval includes column p(i') (since c <= p(i') <= d). But does the tile necessarily contain column p(i')? Yes, because tile covers all columns from c to d. So tile would contain the hole for row i', which is not allowed. Therefore, for any row i' in the tile, we must have p(i') > d (hole to the right). So indeed, if tile contains any left cell for any row, then for all rows in the tile, p(i') > d. That means all holes are to the right of the tile, so tile is left of all holes, thus tile's cells are in L for all rows. So tile is entirely in L. Similarly, if tile contains any right cell for any row, then for that row we have c > p(i). Since c is same for all rows, we get c > p(i) for that row. For any other row i', if p(i') >= c, then tile would contain hole. So we must have p(i') < c for all rows, meaning holes are left of tile, tile is entirely in R. So indeed, tile cannot have mixture: if tile contains any left cell, then all holes must be to the right, else tile would contain a hole. So tile cannot have any row where hole is left of tile (p(i') < c) because then tile would contain hole? Wait, if tile contains any left cell, we have d < p(i). For any other row i', if p(i') < c, then hole is left of tile, but tile's columns are from c to d, which are > p(i')? Actually if p(i') < c, then hole is left of tile, but tile's columns are from c to d, so tile does not contain the hole. That's okay. But does that scenario conflict with the earlier condition d < p(i)? Let's examine: Suppose tile contains left cell for row i, so d < p(i). For another row i', suppose p(i') < c. Then hole is left of tile. Does tile contain any cell in row i' that is left of hole? The tile's columns are from c to d. Since c > p(i'), the tile's columns are all > p(i'), so tile's cells are all to the right of the hole for row i'. So tile's cells are in R_i' for that row. So tile would contain cells in R for some rows and cells in L for other rows. Is that allowed? The earlier argument says tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R. But we see a scenario where tile contains left cell for row i (so tile is left of hole for that row) and for row i' the hole is left of tile, so tile's cells are right of hole for that row. So tile would have cells in both L and R across rows. Does this violate any condition? The tile's cells are all covered, but they are in left region for some rows and right region for others. Is there any problem with that? The solution's claim is that tile must be entirely in L or entirely in R. Let's examine if this scenario can happen given the permutation property.\n\nConsider tile columns [c,d]. For any row i in tile, we need p(i) not in [c,d]. So p(i) either < c or > d. If there exist rows i with p(i) < c and rows i' with p(i') > d, then tile's rows contain holes on both sides. That's allowed. Then tile's cells for rows with p(i) < c are to the right of hole (i.e., in R), and for rows with p(i') > d are to the left of hole (i.e., in L). So tile's cells are mixed.\n\nThe solution's argument tries to show this cannot happen because if tile contains a left cell, then d < p(i). For any other row i', if p(i') < c, then p(i') < c <= d < p(i). So p(i') < d. But does that cause any contradiction? Let's see: tile's columns are [c,d]. Since p(i') < c, p(i') is not in [c,d], so hole is left of tile. That's okay. So tile contains cells in R for that row. No contradiction.\n\nThus the solution's claim that tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R is false. However, we must check if the lower bound argument still holds even if tiles can be mixed. The lower bound argument used the property that each tile has a unique rightmost column for left region. But if a tile can have both left and right cells, does the argument about rightmost column still hold? Let's examine.\n\nThe lower bound for left region considered tiles covering cells in left region. They argued that each tile covering a left cell must have rightmost column equal to the column of that left cell (the rightmost column of tile must be exactly L). But if tile also covers right region cells for other rows, does that affect the argument? The tile's rightmost column is still d. If tile covers left cell (i_L, L) where L = p(i_L)-1, then we have d <= L because tile cannot include column L+1 (hole). Actually they argued that d must equal L because if d < L, then tile would not cover cell (i_L, L). But tile does cover (i_L, L) by assumption. So d must be >= L. But d cannot be > L because then tile would include column L+1 (hole). So d = L. So any tile covering (i_L, L) must have rightmost column exactly L. That holds even if tile also covers right region cells for other rows. So each L requires a tile with rightmost column L. Could a single tile serve multiple L values? No, because each tile has a single rightmost column. So at least n-1 tiles needed to cover left region cells (i_L, L) for each L. However, could a tile covering (i_L, L) also cover (i_M, M) for some other M? If tile's rightmost column is L, then it cannot cover any cell with column > L, so cannot cover (i_M, M) if M > L. If M < L, then tile could cover (i_M, M) as well, but (i_M, M) is leftmost cell of row i_M? Actually (i_M, M) is the rightmost cell of row i_M's left region. For row i_M, left region goes from column 1 to M. So (i_M, M) is the rightmost cell of left region for that row. If M < L, then tile's rightmost column is L > M, so tile includes column M, but does it include (i_M, M)? Possibly if row i_M is within tile's row interval. But tile's rightmost column is L, so tile includes column M. So tile could cover (i_M, M) as well. But the argument that each L requires a distinct tile might be flawed because a tile with rightmost column L could also cover (i_M, M) for M < L. However, the argument is that each L requires at least one tile whose rightmost column equals L. It doesn't claim that each tile can only serve one L; it says a tile can serve at most one value of L because a tile has a single rightmost column. But a tile could serve multiple L values if those L values are less than the tile's rightmost column? Wait, they said \"A tile has a single rightmost column, so a single tile can serve at most one value of L.\" This is inaccurate: a tile with rightmost column L could also serve any L' < L, because the tile's rightmost column is L, but it also contains column L', so it could cover the cell (i_{L'}, L') as well. However, the argument might be that for each L, the tile that covers (i_L, L) must have rightmost column exactly L. So for each L, we need a tile with rightmost column L. But a tile with rightmost column L can also serve other L's? It would have rightmost column L, not L', so it wouldn't count as a tile for L' because the tile's rightmost column is not L'. But the requirement is that for each L, there exists a tile whose rightmost column equals L. So a tile with rightmost column L satisfies the requirement for L, but not for L' (since its rightmost column is not L'). So each L requires a distinct tile with that specific rightmost column. So indeed we need at least n-1 tiles, one for each L.\n\nThus the lower bound argument holds even if tiles can be mixed across left and right region, because the requirement for each L is that there is a tile with rightmost column L. That tile could also cover other left cells, but it still counts as the tile for L. However, we need to ensure that we cannot reuse a tile for multiple L values because the tile's rightmost column is fixed. So we need at least n-1 distinct rightmost columns among tiles covering left region. But could a tile covering left region have rightmost column L1 and also cover left region cell for L2 < L1? Yes, but that tile's rightmost column is L1, not L2. So L2 still needs a tile whose rightmost column equals L2. Could that be the same tile? No, because the tile's rightmost column is L1, not L2. So we need a separate tile for L2. So indeed at least n-1 left tiles.\n\nThus the lower bound argument is valid.\n\nNow check the symmetry for right region: similar argument using leftmost column.\n\nThus lower bound is correct.\n\nNow check the construction: identity permutation p(i)=i. Then left region L_i = columns 1 to i-1, right region R_i = columns i+1 to n. The construction uses vertical tiles for each column j: left tile T^L_j = {(i,j) | i=j+1,...,n}. That covers column j for rows below diagonal (i > j). Right tile T^R_j = {(i,j) | i=1,...,j-1}. That covers column j for rows above diagonal (i < j). These are vertical tiles (height varies). They are disjoint because left tile uses rows > j, right tile uses rows < j. The diagonal cell (j,j) is uncovered. So each column j has a left tile covering rows below diagonal and a right tile covering rows above diagonal. For column j=1, left tile T^L_1 covers rows 2..n, right tile T^R_1? Actually j runs from 2 to n for right tiles. So column 1 has only left tile; column n has only right tile? Let's check: left tiles for j=1..n-1, right tiles for j=2..n. So column 1 has left tile covering rows 2..n, column n has right tile covering rows 1..n-1. The diagonal cells (i,i) are uncovered. So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2.\n\nCheck that each row i has exactly one uncovered cell (i,i). Row i: left tile for column j < i covers cell (i,j) if j < i? Actually left tile T^L_j covers rows > j. For row i, which left tiles cover it? For each column j < i, we have j+1 <= i, so row i is included in left tile T^L_j. So row i is covered by left tiles for all columns j < i. Similarly, right tiles for columns j > i cover row i? Right tile T^R_j covers rows 1..j-1. For j > i, i <= j-1, so row i is covered by right tile T^R_j. So row i is covered by left tiles for columns < i and right tiles for columns > i. So all cells in row i except column i are covered. So each row has exactly one uncovered cell at column i. Similarly each column j: left tile covers rows > j, right tile covers rows < j, leaving row j uncovered. So each column has exactly one uncovered cell at row j. So the construction works.\n\nThus the answer 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to check if any issues regarding construction details: The solution provides explicit description of tiles. That's concrete: vertical tile definitions. So it's Category C (explicit specification). So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow check if any presentation issues: The solution is fairly rigorous. Might have minor gaps: The argument that tile lies entirely in L or R is not fully justified, but they gave reasoning. However, we found that claim is false in general, but they used it to derive lower bound. However, the lower bound argument may still be valid even if the claim is false? Let's examine more carefully: They used the claim to argue that left tiles and right tiles are disjoint. Actually they used it to argue that left-tiles and right-tiles are disjoint. But even if a tile could be mixed, left and right tiles would not be disjoint? Let's examine: If a tile can have both left and right cells, then it would be both a left tile and a right tile? The solution defines left-tiles as those lying in L, right-tiles as those lying in R. If a tile can be mixed, then it's neither purely left nor purely right, so classification fails. But they claim left-tiles and right-tiles are disjoint. If there exist mixed tiles, they would not be counted as left or right, but they could be counted as something else. However, the lower bound argument uses left-tiles to cover left region and right-tiles to cover right region. If a tile is mixed, it could cover cells in both left and right region, potentially reducing the number of tiles needed. So the lower bound might be invalid if mixed tiles are allowed.\n\nThus we need to examine if mixed tiles are indeed possible given the constraints: each row and column must have exactly one uncovered cell. The holes are at positions (i,p(i)). The tile must avoid covering any hole. So a tile can have rows where holes are left of tile and rows where holes are right of tile. That seems possible. Let's try to construct an example for a small n, say n=3, with permutation p = (2,3,1) (i.e., holes at (1,2), (2,3), (3,1)). Could we have a tile covering rows 1 and 2, columns 2 to 2 (i.e., column 2)? Let's see: tile covering rows 1-2, column 2. Row 1 hole at column 2, tile would cover hole, not allowed. So tile cannot include column equal to any hole for any row.\n\nTry tile covering rows 1-2, columns 1-1 (column 1). Row 1 hole at column 2 >1, so tile is left of hole (good). Row 2 hole at column 3 >1, tile is left of hole (good). So tile covers cells (1,1) and (2,1). That's left region for both rows. So tile is left tile.\n\nTry tile covering rows 2-3, columns 2-2 (column 2). Row 2 hole at column 3 >2, tile left of hole. Row 3 hole at column 1 <2, tile right of hole. So tile includes row 3 where hole is left of tile, so tile's cells for row 3 are right region. So tile includes left region for row 2 and right region for row 3. So tile is mixed. Does this violate any condition? It does not cover any hole: row 2 hole at col 3 not covered, row 3 hole at col 1 not covered. So tile is allowed. So indeed mixed tiles are possible.\n\nThus the claim that any tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R is false. So the lower bound argument that left-tiles and right-tiles are disjoint may be flawed because there could be tiles that are neither left nor right, but they could reduce the number of tiles needed. However, the lower bound argument uses the fact that each L length L requires a tile with rightmost column L. That argument does not rely on the claim that tiles are purely left; it only uses that any tile covering (i_L, L) must have rightmost column L. That's still true even for mixed tiles. However, the existence of mixed tiles could allow a tile to cover multiple left cells with different rightmost columns? No, a tile's rightmost column is fixed. So each L still needs a tile with rightmost column L. So the lower bound holds even if mixed tiles exist.\n\nBut the lower bound also uses that left-tiles and right-tiles are disjoint to sum up counts: (n-1)+(n-1). If there are mixed tiles, they could count as both left and right? Actually they would be counted as neither left nor right, but they could cover cells in both L and R, potentially reducing the total number of tiles needed below 2n-2. However, the lower bound argument for right region also uses a similar reasoning: each right region length M requires a tile with leftmost column something. Mixed tiles could also serve both left and right region requirements simultaneously, maybe reducing the total count. Let's examine: For right region, they argue each row i has right region length n-p(i). For each length M, there is a unique row i_M with that length. Then any tile covering the cell (i_M, p(i_M)+M) (the leftmost cell of the right region for that row) must have leftmost column exactly p(i_M)+1? Actually they argue using leftmost column. So each M requires a tile with leftmost column something. Mixed tiles could have leftmost column = something and rightmost column = something else. Could a single tile serve both a left region requirement (rightmost column L) and a right region requirement (leftmost column something)? Possibly if tile's leftmost column = something for right region and rightmost column = L for left region. Then tile could serve both a left region L and a right region M simultaneously. That would reduce total count below 2n-2. So the lower bound of 2n-2 might be too high if mixed tiles are allowed.\n\nThus we need to examine if the lower bound argument indeed holds even with mixed tiles. The solution's argument for left region lower bound does not consider that a tile could also serve a right region requirement. However, they later sum left and right lower bounds assuming disjointness. If mixed tiles exist, they could be counted in both lower bounds, but they are the same tile, so total number of tiles could be less than sum of the two lower bounds. However, they claim left-tiles and right-tiles are disjoint, which is false. So the lower bound of 2n-2 may not be valid.\n\nThus we need to verify if the minimal number of tiles is indeed 2n-2 or could be lower. Let's test small n to see if we can achieve fewer than 2n-2 tiles. For n=2, grid 2x2. Need each row and column have exactly one uncovered square. So we need exactly two uncovered squares, one per row and column, thus they must be at positions (1,1) and (2,2) or (1,2) and (2,1). Let's try to cover the other squares with tiles. There are 4 squares total, 2 uncovered, 2 covered. Tiles must be rectangles covering unit squares, cannot overlap. The covered squares are the other two squares. Could we cover them with a single tile? The two covered squares are either (1,2) and (2,2) if uncovered are (1,1),(2,1) (holes on column 1). Those two squares form a vertical 2x1 rectangle (rows 1-2, column 2). That's a tile. So we can cover with 1 tile. 2n-2 = 2*2-2 = 2. But we achieved 1 tile. So minimal number of tiles for n=2 is 1, not 2. Let's check constraints: Each row must have exactly one uncovered square. In this arrangement, uncovered squares are (1,1) and (2,1). Row 1 has uncovered (1,1), row 2 has uncovered (2,1). Column 1 has uncovered squares (1,1) and (2,1) → two uncovered squares in column 1, violating column condition (each column must have exactly one uncovered square). So that arrangement is invalid. Let's try uncovered squares (1,1) and (2,2). Then covered squares are (1,2) and (2,1). Those are not adjacent, they form a \"checkerboard\" pattern. They cannot be covered by a single rectangle because they are not contiguous. So we need two tiles: each covering a single cell. So minimal number of tiles is 2. So 2n-2 = 2 works for n=2.\n\nNow n=3. Let's try to see if we can achieve fewer than 2n-2 = 4 tiles. The solution says minimal is 4. Let's test if we can achieve 3 tiles. We need 3 uncovered squares (one per row, one per column). So holes form a permutation. Suppose we choose identity permutation (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Then covered cells are all off-diagonal: 6 cells. Can we cover them with 3 tiles? Possibly using two vertical tiles for columns 1 and 3, and one horizontal tile for row 2? Let's try to find a covering with 3 rectangles without overlapping and not covering holes. The off-diagonal cells are:\n\nRow1: columns 2,3\nRow2: columns 1,3\nRow3: columns 1,2\n\nWe need to cover these 6 cells with 3 rectangles. Each rectangle must be axis-aligned and cover contiguous rows and columns. Let's try:\n\nTile A: rows 1-3, column 2? That would cover (1,2),(2,2),(3,2). But (2,2) is a hole (since identity). So cannot include column 2 across all rows. Could restrict rows: maybe tile covering (1,2) and (2,2) not allowed. So column 2 cannot be used across row 2. So we need to avoid covering holes.\n\nConsider tile covering (1,2) and (1,3) (row 1, columns 2-3). That's a horizontal tile covering row 1's two cells. That's allowed because row 1 hole at column 1, so columns 2-3 are fine. Tile B covering (2,1) and (3,1) (column 1 rows 2-3). That's vertical tile covering column 1 rows 2-3. Row 2 hole at column 2, row 3 hole at column 3, so column 1 is fine. Tile C covering (2,3) and (3,2)? That's not a rectangle: they are not aligned. Could we cover (2,3) and (3,2) with a rectangle? The rectangle covering rows 2-3 and columns 2-3 includes cells (2,2) and (3,3) which are holes. So not allowed. So we need separate tiles for (2,3) and (3,2). That would be two more tiles, total 4. So minimal is 4.\n\nBut maybe another permutation yields a covering with 3 tiles. Let's try permutation (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Holes: (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Covered cells:\n\nRow1: columns 1,3\nRow2: columns 1,2\nRow3: columns 2,3\n\nWe need to cover these 6 cells. Could we use tile covering (1,1),(2,1) vertical column 1 rows 1-2. That's allowed (hole at (3,1) not covered). Tile covering (2,2),(3,2) vertical column 2 rows 2-3. Hole at (1,2) not covered. Tile covering (1,3),(3,3) not contiguous rows (1 and 3). Could use tile covering (1,3) alone and (3,3) alone, making total 4 tiles. Could we use tile covering (1,3),(2,2),(3,3) as a shape? Not a rectangle.\n\nTry permutation (1,3),(2,1),(3,2). Holes: (1,3),(2,1),(3,2). Covered:\n\nRow1: columns 1,2\nRow2: columns 2,3\nRow3: columns 1,3\n\nWe need to cover these 6 cells. Could we use tile covering (1,1),(1,2) horizontal row1. Tile covering (2,2),(2,3) horizontal row2. Tile covering (3,1),(3,3) cannot be a rectangle because columns not contiguous. Could use vertical tile covering (1,1),(3,1) column1 rows1-3 includes (2,1) which is a hole, not allowed. So need separate tile for (3,1) and (3,3). So 4 tiles.\n\nThus minimal for n=3 appears to be 4 = 2n-2. So lower bound seems tight for small n.\n\nBut we need to consider possibility of mixed tiles reducing count for larger n. Let's test n=4. Lower bound 2n-2 = 6. Could we achieve 5? Let's try to find a covering with 5 tiles. Might be possible? Let's attempt to design a pattern.\n\nWe need a permutation of holes. Let's try identity permutation (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4). Off-diagonal cells: 12 cells. We need to cover them with 5 rectangles. Each rectangle can cover many cells. The solution uses vertical tiles for each column j: left tile covering rows > j, right tile covering rows < j. That's 6 tiles (2 per column except extremes). Could we combine some left and right tiles into a single mixed tile? For example, tile covering rows 2-3, columns 2-3? Let's see: rows 2-3, columns 2-3 includes cells (2,2) and (3,3) which are holes, not allowed. So cannot cross diagonal.\n\nBut maybe we can use a tile that covers left region cells for some rows and right region cells for other rows, as we saw earlier. For identity permutation, left region for row i is columns 1..i-1, right region columns i+1..4. Could we have a tile covering rows 2-3, columns 2? That would be column 2 rows 2-3. Row2 hole at column2, so tile would cover hole, not allowed. So any tile that includes column equal to row index for any row in its row interval is not allowed. So tile's column interval must avoid the diagonal entries for rows in its interval. For identity permutation, diagonal entries are (i,i). So tile's column interval must avoid column i for each row i in its interval. So tile's column interval must be either entirely left of all those rows' diagonal columns (i.e., columns < min row index in interval) or entirely right of all those rows' diagonal columns (columns > max row index in interval). Because if we have rows a..b, the diagonal columns are a..b. So to avoid covering any hole, the tile's column interval must be either entirely < a (left of all holes) or entirely > b (right of all holes). So tile cannot be mixed across left and right region for identity permutation. So for identity permutation, the claim that tile lies entirely left or right holds. So lower bound is tight.\n\nBut for other permutations, maybe mixed tiles are possible. The lower bound argument uses any permutation p. However, the construction achieving bound uses identity permutation, which satisfies the property that any tile must be wholly left or right. For lower bound, they used any permutation to argue that at least n-1 left tiles and n-1 right tiles are needed. But if there exists a permutation where mixed tiles can reduce the total number of tiles below 2n-2, then the lower bound would not be universal; the minimal number of tiles might be lower than 2n-2. However, the problem asks for the minimum number of tiles needed to achieve the condition. So we need to find the minimal possible across all permutations and tile placements. The solution shows that for identity permutation we can achieve 2n-2. But we need to check if there exists a placement with fewer tiles using a different permutation.\n\nThus we need to examine if the lower bound 2n-2 holds for any permutation, i.e., it's a universal lower bound regardless of permutation. The solution's lower bound argument attempted to prove that for any permutation p, we need at least n-1 left tiles and n-1 right tiles, thus at least 2n-2 tiles. However, we found that the claim that any tile lies entirely in L or entirely in R is false. So the lower bound argument might be flawed. However, maybe the lower bound can be salvaged by a different argument: they used the fact that each L_i length is distinct, and each L length L requires a tile with rightmost column L. That argument does not rely on tile being wholly left; it only uses that any tile covering (i_L, L) must have rightmost column L. That holds regardless of tile being mixed. So we still get at least n-1 tiles that have rightmost column L for each L. Similarly, for right region we get at least n-1 tiles that have leftmost column something. However, could a single tile satisfy both a left region requirement (rightmost column L) and a right region requirement (leftmost column something) simultaneously? Yes, a tile could have rightmost column L and leftmost column something else (maybe > something). For right region requirement, we need a tile that covers a cell (i_M, p(i_M)+M) which is the leftmost cell of right region for that row. That tile must have leftmost column equal to p(i_M)+1 (the leftmost column of right region). So a tile that has leftmost column = p(i_M)+1 and rightmost column = L for some L. If we can choose a tile such that its leftmost column matches a required leftmost column for some right region, and its rightmost column matches a required rightmost column for some left region, then that tile would count for both a left and a right requirement, reducing total count.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 might not hold if we can pair left and right requirements in a single tile. However, can we pair them? Let's examine the constraints: For a tile covering a left region cell (i_L, L) and also covering a right region cell (i_M, p(i_M)+1) (the leftmost cell of right region for row i_M). For tile to cover both, the tile's column interval must include both column L and column p(i_M)+1. Since tile's column interval is contiguous, we need L >= p(i_M)+1 or L <= p(i_M)+1. Actually if L is the rightmost column, then L >= p(i_M)+1. The tile's leftmost column is some c <= L. For the tile to have leftmost column equal to p(i_M)+1 (to satisfy right region requirement), we need c = p(i_M)+1. So tile's column interval is [p(i_M)+1, L]. Then the tile's rightmost column is L, leftmost column is p(i_M)+1. So tile would satisfy both requirements: rightmost column L (covers left region cell) and leftmost column p(i_M)+1 (covers right region cell). However, does such a tile avoid covering holes for all rows it covers? It must avoid holes for each row in its row interval. Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we choose rows a..b. For each row i in that interval, we need p(i) not in [c,d] = [p(i_M)+1, L]. So for each row i, either p(i) < p(i_M)+1 or p(i) > L. So p(i) must be either less than leftmost column or greater than rightmost column. That means for each row i, the hole is either left of tile or right of tile. So the tile can have rows where holes are left and rows where holes are right. That's allowed. So a mixed tile can indeed satisfy both left and right region requirements.\n\nThus the lower bound argument that left and right tiles are disjoint is false. So the minimal number of tiles might be less than 2n-2.\n\nWe need to check if we can achieve fewer tiles for n=2025. Let's try to find a construction with fewer than 2n-2 tiles. Perhaps we can pair left and right requirements to reduce count by about half? Let's explore.\n\nGoal: cover all cells except a permutation of holes. We want to minimize number of rectangles covering the rest. This is reminiscent of covering a permutation matrix complement with rectangles. This is like the problem of covering the complement of a permutation matrix (i.e., all cells except one per row and column) with axis-aligned rectangles that avoid the holes. Equivalent to covering a \"grid graph\" minus a permutation of vertices with rectangles. This is similar to the problem of covering a rook board with non-attacking rooks? Actually not.\n\nWe need to cover the complement of a permutation matrix (size n x n) with axis-aligned rectangles that avoid the permutation points. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover the complement of a permutation matrix is known as the \"rectangular covering number\" of the complement of a permutation matrix. This is related to the concept of \"grid covering number\" or \"rectangular partition\". There's known results: The complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into 2n-2 monotone rectangles (like the solution's construction). But can it be done with fewer? Let's think.\n\nWe can think of the complement as a union of two monotone regions: the cells left of the permutation and right of the permutation. The complement is the union of those two \"staircase\" shapes. The minimal number of axis-aligned rectangles needed to partition a monotone shape (like a Young diagram) is equal to the number of \"corners\" or something. For a permutation shape, the left region is a Young diagram shape (Ferrers diagram) defined by the permutation. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram is equal to the number of rows (or columns) minus the number of \"inner corners\"? Actually there is known result: The rectangle covering number of a Young diagram (Ferrers shape) equals the number of rows (or columns) of the diagram's \"profile\"? Not sure. But there is known concept of \"monotone matrix\" and \"covering by rectangles\". The complement of a permutation matrix consists of two Young diagrams (the cells left of the permutation and the cells right of the permutation). The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition each Young diagram is equal to the number of distinct row lengths (or column lengths). For a permutation, the left region's row lengths are ℓ_i = p(i)-1. The number of distinct positive lengths is n-1 (since ℓ_i are 0,...,n-1). So the left region requires at least n-1 rectangles to partition (if we require rectangles that are axis-aligned and cannot cross holes). Similarly, the right region requires at least n-1 rectangles. So total at least 2n-2. However, this lower bound is for partitioning each region separately, not allowing rectangles to cover cells from both regions. But can a rectangle cover cells from both left and right region? That would cross the diagonal shape, but must avoid holes. As we argued, a rectangle can have rows where holes are left and rows where holes are right, thus covering cells from both sides. So a rectangle can cover cells from both left and right region simultaneously, but it cannot cross a hole within a given row. So it can only include columns that are either all less than p(i) for rows where holes are right, or all greater than p(i) for rows where holes are left. So a rectangle can be \"mixed\" across rows.\n\nThus the lower bound of n-1 for each region separately may not hold when mixing is allowed. However, the argument that each distinct left length L requires a tile with rightmost column L still holds regardless of mixing. So we still need at least n-1 tiles that have rightmost column L for each L. Similarly, each distinct right length M requires a tile with leftmost column something. But a tile can satisfy both a left requirement and a right requirement simultaneously if its rightmost column is L and leftmost column is something else. But the leftmost column requirement for right region is that the tile's leftmost column equals some value (call it C). So a tile can have rightmost column L and leftmost column C simultaneously. So it can satisfy both a left requirement (L) and a right requirement (C). So we need at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1 tiles? Actually we need at least n-1 distinct rightmost columns among tiles covering left region cells, and at least n-1 distinct leftmost columns among tiles covering right region cells. If a tile can have both a rightmost column L and leftmost column C, then each tile contributes one rightmost column and one leftmost column. So we need at least n-1 tiles to provide n-1 distinct rightmost columns, and also at least n-1 tiles to provide n-1 distinct leftmost columns. If each tile provides one of each, then we need at least n-1 tiles, but we also need to ensure that the set of leftmost columns across tiles covers all required leftmost columns. Since each tile can have a distinct leftmost column, we could potentially have n-1 tiles that each have a distinct leftmost column and a distinct rightmost column. So the lower bound could be n-1, not 2n-2. But we need to check if it's possible to have a set of n-1 tiles that simultaneously cover all left region cells and all right region cells. That seems unlikely because each tile can only cover a rectangular region, and covering all left region cells might require each tile's rightmost column to be distinct, but also each tile's leftmost column must be distinct for right region. So we need a set of n-1 tiles where the rightmost columns are all distinct values from 1 to n-1, and leftmost columns are all distinct values from 2 to n (maybe). Could we assign each tile a pair (c,d) such that c = something, d = something else, covering all left and right cells? Possibly we can pair each L with a C such that tile covers left region cells up to column L and right region cells from column C onward for appropriate rows. But can a single tile cover both left region cells for some rows and right region cells for other rows? Yes, as we argued. So maybe we can achieve n-1 tiles total. Let's test for small n to see if we can achieve n-1 tiles.\n\nFor n=2, n-1 = 1. Indeed we can cover the complement of a permutation matrix with 1 tile? Let's test n=2 with identity permutation holes (1,1) and (2,2). Complement cells: (1,2) and (2,1). Can we cover both with a single rectangle? The rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 1-2 includes holes, not allowed. Any rectangle covering both cells must include either (1,1) or (2,2). So not possible. So need 2 tiles. So lower bound of n-1 is not sufficient for n=2. So maybe the lower bound of 2n-2 is correct for n=2. But maybe for larger n, we can do better than 2n-2 but not as low as n-1.\n\nLet's test n=3. n-1 = 2. Can we cover complement with 2 tiles? Complement has 6 cells. Could we cover them with 2 rectangles? Let's try to find a permutation and tile arrangement.\n\nPermutation identity: complement cells are off-diagonal. Could we cover them with 2 rectangles? Possibly one rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 1-2? That would include (2,2) which is a hole. Not allowed. Another rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 2-3 includes (2,2) and (3,3). Not allowed. So not possible.\n\nTry permutation (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Complement cells: as earlier. Could we cover them with 2 rectangles? Let's attempt: rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 1-2 includes (1,2) hole? Actually (1,2) is a hole. So not allowed. Try rectangle covering rows 2-3, columns 2-3 includes (2,3) hole. Not allowed. So not possible.\n\nThus minimal for n=3 is 4, which is 2n-2.\n\nNow n=4. Could we achieve 5 tiles? Let's try to find a permutation and tile arrangement that uses 5 tiles. Let's attempt to design a mixed tile arrangement that reduces count. For identity permutation, we need 6 tiles as per solution. But maybe another permutation allows fewer. Let's try permutation p = (2,1,4,3). So holes: (1,2),(2,1),(3,4),(4,3). Complement cells: all other cells.\n\nLet's list grid:\n\nRows/Columns:\n\nRow1: columns 1,3,4 (hole at 2)\nRow2: columns 2,3,4 (hole at 1)\nRow3: columns 1,2,3 (hole at 4)\nRow4: columns 1,2,4 (hole at 3)\n\nWe need to cover these 12 cells with rectangles.\n\nGoal: try to use 5 rectangles. Let's see if we can partition complement into 5 rectangles.\n\nOne approach: Use vertical tiles for column 1 rows 2-4? Column 1 rows 2-4 includes (2,1) hole? Actually (2,1) is a hole, so cannot include row2 column1. So column1 rows 3-4 is okay (cells (3,1),(4,1)). That's a vertical tile covering two cells.\n\nColumn 4 rows 1-2 includes (1,4),(2,4). Holes at (1,2) and (2,1) not in column4, so okay. So vertical tile covering column4 rows 1-2.\n\nNow we have left cells: (1,1) (hole at (1,2) so (1,1) is left region), (1,3) (right region), (2,2) (right region), (2,3) (right region), (3,2) (left region), (3,3) (left region), (4,2) (left region), (4,4) (right region). Actually let's list all cells not covered yet:\n\nCovered so far: (3,1),(4,1) and (1,4),(2,4). So remaining cells:\n\nRow1: columns 1,3 (since column2 is hole, column4 covered)\nRow2: columns 2,3 (col1 hole, col4 covered)\nRow3: columns 2,3 (col1 covered, col4 hole)\nRow4: columns 2,4 (col1 covered, col3 hole)\n\nNow we need to cover these 8 cells with 3 more tiles (to total 5). Let's see if we can do that.\n\nConsider rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 2-3: includes cells (1,2) hole, (2,2) okay, (1,3) okay, (2,3) okay. But includes hole (1,2) which is a hole, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nConsider rectangle covering rows 3-4, columns 2-3: includes (3,2),(3,3),(4,2),(4,3). (4,3) is a hole, not allowed.\n\nConsider rectangle covering rows 1-4, columns 2-2 (column2 rows 1-4). Column2 cells: (1,2) hole, (2,2) okay, (3,2) okay, (4,2) okay. Contains hole (1,2) -> not allowed.\n\nConsider rectangle covering rows 1-4, columns 3-3 (column3). Cells: (1,3) okay, (2,3) okay, (3,3) okay, (4,3) hole. Contains hole (4,3) -> not allowed.\n\nConsider rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 1-1 (column1 rows1-3). Contains (1,1) okay, (2,1) hole, (3,1) covered already. Not allowed.\n\nConsider rectangle covering rows 2-4, columns 2-2 (column2 rows2-4). Contains (2,2) okay, (3,2) okay, (4,2) okay. No holes? Actually (2,2) is not a hole, (3,2) not a hole, (4,2) not a hole. So column2 rows2-4 is a vertical tile covering three cells. That is allowed because (2,2) is not a hole (hole at row2 col1). So we can place tile covering (2,2),(3,2),(4,2). Good.\n\nNow remaining cells: (1,1),(1,3),(2,3),(3,3),(4,4). Also (3,3) is left region? Actually (3,3) is not a hole (hole at (3,4)). So (3,3) is left region. (4,4) is right region (hole at (4,3)). So we have 5 cells left.\n\nWe have used 3 tiles so far (col1 rows3-4, col4 rows1-2, col2 rows2-4). Need to cover remaining 5 cells with 2 tiles to reach total 5. Let's see if possible.\n\nRemaining cells positions:\n- (1,1)\n- (1,3)\n- (2,3)\n- (3,3)\n- (4,4)\n\nWe can try rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 1-3? That includes many holes: (1,2) hole, (2,1) hole, (3,4) not in region. So not allowed.\n\nTry rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 3-3 (column3 rows1-2): cells (1,3),(2,3). Both are okay (hole at (1,2) and (2,1) not in column3). So tile covering (1,3),(2,3) is allowed.\n\nNow remaining cells: (1,1),(3,3),(4,4). Could we cover (1,1) and (3,3) with a rectangle? They are not aligned. Could we cover (3,3) and (4,4) with a rectangle? That would be rows 3-4, columns 3-4 includes (3,4) hole, (4,3) hole. Not allowed. Could we cover (1,1) and (4,4) with rectangle rows1-4, columns1-4 includes holes. Not allowed.\n\nThus we need at least 3 more tiles for those 3 cells (each as a single tile). So total tiles = 6 (col1 rows3-4, col4 rows1-2, col2 rows2-4, col3 rows1-2, plus three singletons) = 7 > 5. So not good.\n\nThus maybe 5 tiles is not possible for n=4. Let's try systematic approach: The lower bound 2n-2 = 6 for n=4. We suspect minimal is 6. Let's try to see if any arrangement can achieve 5. Could we find a construction with 5? Let's attempt to design a permutation that yields a shape where left region and right region can be covered by fewer rectangles by mixing. Possibly we can reduce by 1 tile for each pair of lengths? Let's think.\n\nGeneral shape: For each L (distinct left length), we need a tile with rightmost column L. For each M (distinct right length), we need a tile with leftmost column something. If we can pair each L with a distinct M such that a single tile has rightmost column L and leftmost column something that matches a required leftmost column for some M, we can combine them. So we could reduce number of tiles by at most min(n-1, n-1) = n-1, but we need to pair them. However, we need to ensure that the tile can cover the required cells for both left and right region simultaneously. That might be possible if we choose permutation such that the required leftmost columns for right region are exactly the set {L+1} for each L? Let's examine.\n\nFor each row i, left length ℓ_i = p(i)-1, right length r_i = n-p(i). The leftmost column of right region for row i is p(i)+1 = ℓ_i+2. So the set of leftmost columns for right region is {ℓ_i+2 | i=1..n}. Since ℓ_i runs over 0..n-1, ℓ_i+2 runs over 2..n+1? Actually ℓ_i ∈ {0,...,n-1}. So ℓ_i+2 ∈ {2,...,n+1}. But p(i) ∈ {1,...,n}, so ℓ_i+2 = p(i)+1 ∈ {2,...,n+1}. However, p(i)+1 = n+1 for p(i)=n, which is n+1 (outside grid). But for the row where p(i)=n, the right region length is 0, so there is no leftmost column for right region (since no right cells). So we consider only rows with p(i) < n, i.e., ℓ_i ≤ n-2. Then leftmost column = p(i)+1 ∈ {2,...,n}. So the set of leftmost columns for right region (excluding rows where right region empty) is {2,...,n} (each appears exactly once except maybe some missing? Let's check: Since p(i) runs over all values 1..n, p(i)+1 runs over 2..n+1, but p(i)=n yields p(i)+1=n+1 which is not a column; that row has no right region. So the set of leftmost columns for right region is {2,...,n} (each appears exactly once except maybe one missing if p(i)=n? Actually p(i)=n corresponds to leftmost column n+1 which is not a column, so we have n-1 leftmost columns: 2..n). So there are n-1 distinct leftmost columns needed.\n\nThus we have n-1 distinct rightmost columns needed (L values 1..n-1) and n-1 distinct leftmost columns needed (C values 2..n). So we need to assign to each tile a pair (c,d) where c ∈ {2,...,n} and d ∈ {1,...,n-1}. We need to cover each required L and each required C. If we can pair each L with a distinct C, we could have n-1 tiles each covering both a left requirement (rightmost column L) and a right requirement (leftmost column C). But we need to ensure that the tile can actually cover the required cells for those rows. For each L, there is a unique row i_L such that ℓ_i = L (i.e., p(i)=L+1). For each C, there is a unique row i_C' such that p(i)+1 = C (i.e., p(i) = C-1). So the row for left requirement L is i_L with p(i_L) = L+1. The row for right requirement C is i_C' with p(i_C') = C-1. So we need a tile that covers cell (i_L, L) (the rightmost left cell of row i_L) and also covers cell (i_C', C) (the leftmost right cell of row i_C'). The tile's column interval must be [c,d] where c = C and d = L. For this to be a valid interval, we need C ≤ L. So we need to pair each L with a C such that C ≤ L. Since C ranges from 2..n and L ranges from 1..n-1, we can pair each L with C = L+1? That would give C > L, not allowed. But we can pair L with C = L (if C = L, then C ∈ {2,...,n} and L ∈ {1,...,n-1}. The intersection is {2,...,n-1}. So we could pair L with C = L for L=2..n-1. For L=1, C cannot be 1 (C min is 2). For L=n-1, C can be n-1. So we could pair all L from 2..n-1 with C = L. That leaves L=1 and C=n unpaired. For L=1, we need a tile with rightmost column 1. For C=n, we need a tile with leftmost column n. Could we combine L=1 and C=n in a single tile? That would require C = n ≤ L = 1, not possible. So we need at least two extra tiles for those extremes. So total tiles needed would be (n-2) tiles for the paired L's (2..n-2?), plus 2 extra tiles for L=1 and C=n, plus maybe one tile for L=n-1? Wait, let's systematically count.\n\nWe have L values: 1,2,...,n-1 (n-1 values). C values: 2,3,...,n (n-1 values). We want to assign each L to a tile; each tile can also satisfy a C (maybe same tile). We need to cover all L and all C. Each tile can cover at most one L (its rightmost column) and at most one C (its leftmost column). So each tile can cover at most two requirements: one L and one C. So the minimal number of tiles needed is at least ceil((#L + #C)/2) = ceil((2(n-1))/2) = n-1. So lower bound is n-1. However, we also need to ensure that for each tile, the leftmost column is ≤ rightmost column (c ≤ d). So we need to find a matching between L and C such that each pair (C,L) satisfies C ≤ L. This is a bipartite matching problem: we have L values sorted ascending, C values sorted ascending. We need to pair each L with a C ≤ L (or possibly leave some unpaired). This is like we need to match each C to some L that is at least C. Since there are as many C as L, we can try to assign each C to a distinct L with L ≥ C. Since L values go from 1 to n-1, C values go from 2 to n. For C=n, we need L ≥ n, but max L = n-1, so impossible. So C=n cannot be paired with any L. So C=n must be a separate tile (with leftmost column n). That tile would have leftmost column n, rightmost column maybe something else? But if leftmost column = n, then rightmost column must be ≥ leftmost column, so rightmost column = n as well (since max column is n). So tile would be a vertical tile at column n covering some rows where holes are left of column n (i.e., rows where p(i) < n). But row where p(i) = n has no right region, but tile can cover rows where p(i) < n (holes left of column n). That's okay. So we need a tile covering column n for those rows. That tile will have rightmost column n, which also satisfies a leftmost column requirement for L=n? Actually L=n is not a requirement; L max is n-1. So this tile only satisfies C=n requirement, not any L. So we need at least one tile for C=n.\n\nNow for L=1, we need a tile with rightmost column 1. Its leftmost column must be ≤ 1, so leftmost column = 1. So tile covering column 1. That tile also satisfies C=1? But C values start at 2, so no. So tile for L=1 is separate.\n\nThus we need at least 2 extra tiles for extremes L=1 and C=n. For the remaining L values 2..n-1 and C values 2..n-1, we can pair each L with C = L (or any C ≤ L). So we can have n-2 tiles covering both a left and a right requirement each. So total tiles = (n-2) + 2 = n. Wait, we have n-2 paired tiles + 2 separate = n. But we also need to cover the right requirement for C=2? Actually C=2 is paired with L=2, okay. So total tiles = n. But we need to cover all L values and all C values. Let's count: L values count = n-1. We have one tile for L=1 (covers L=1). For each L from 2 to n-1, we pair it with C = L (so each tile covers both L and C). That's (n-2) tiles. Then we have tile for C=n (covers C=n). So total tiles = 1 + (n-2) + 1 = n. So we get n tiles total. That's one more than n-1, but less than 2n-2. So maybe minimal number of tiles is n? Let's test for n=2: n=2, predicted minimal tiles = 2? Actually n=2 gives n=2, but we found minimal is 2 (consistent). For n=3, predicted minimal = 3? But we found minimal is 4. So maybe the pairing scheme fails for small n due to constraints like L=1 cannot be paired, C=n cannot be paired, leaving n tiles. For n=3, n=3, but we found minimal 4. So maybe the pairing scheme cannot be realized due to additional constraints (like tile covering both left and right cells may not be possible for some rows). Let's test n=3 with pairing scheme: L values = {1,2}, C values = {2,3}. Pair L=2 with C=2 (tile covering column interval [2,2]?). Actually tile with leftmost column 2, rightmost column 2 (single column). It must cover cell (i_L, L) = (i_2,2) where i_2 is row with p(i)=3 (since L=2 => p(i)=L+1=3). So row i_2 has hole at column 3. So left region cell (i_2,2) is left of hole. Tile column interval [2,2] includes column 2 only, which is left of hole for row i_2. For rows where p(i) = 2 (hole at column 2), tile column 2 would be hole, not allowed. So tile cannot include any row where p(i) = 2. So tile can only include rows where holes are either left of column 2 (p(i) < 2) or right of column 2 (p(i) > 2). Since p(i) values are 1,2,3. For p(i)=1, hole at column 1, tile column 2 is right of hole, okay. For p(i)=3, hole at column 3, tile column 2 is left of hole, okay. So tile can include rows with p(i)=1 and p(i)=3. That includes rows i_1 (hole at column 1) and i_2 (hole at column 3). So tile covering column 2 rows i_1 and i_2 would be allowed. So tile covering column 2 rows where holes are not at column 2 (i.e., rows 1 and 3 if p(1)=? Actually we need to assign permutation such that p(i) values are 1,2,3. Let's choose identity permutation p(i)=i. Then p(1)=1, p(2)=2, p(3)=3. Then L values: ℓ_1=0, ℓ_2=1, ℓ_3=2. So L=2 corresponds to row i=3 (hole at column 3). C values: C=2 corresponds to row i=1 (hole at column 1). Actually C = p(i)+1, so C=2 corresponds to p(i)=1 => row 1. So tile covering column 2 rows 1 and 3 would include row 2? Row 2 has hole at column 2, which would be covered, not allowed. So tile cannot include row 2. So tile can include rows 1 and 3, which is okay, but tile's row interval must be contiguous. Rows 1 and 3 are not contiguous (they skip row 2). So tile cannot include both rows 1 and 3 because tile rows must be an interval. So tile cannot cover both rows 1 and 3 without also covering row 2, which would be invalid. So tile covering column 2 cannot satisfy both left requirement (L=2) and right requirement (C=2) simultaneously because the rows needed are not contiguous. So the pairing scheme fails due to contiguity constraints.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 may be correct after all, because the contiguity of rows restricts the ability to pair left and right requirements. The solution's argument that each L requires a distinct tile with rightmost column L is valid, but we need to also consider that the tile covering L must also have a contiguous row interval. However, the lower bound argument does not rely on contiguity beyond that each tile has a rightmost column L; it doesn't require that tile also covers the right requirement. So the lower bound is still valid: at least n-1 left tiles. Similarly at least n-1 right tiles. But could a tile be counted as both a left tile and a right tile? The solution's claim that left-tiles and right-tiles are disjoint prevents that. But we found that a tile could be mixed, not purely left or right. However, does a mixed tile count as a left tile or a right tile? The solution defines left-tiles as those lying in L, right-tiles as those lying in R. Mixed tile does not lie entirely in L or R, so it's not counted as either. So the lower bound argument that there are at least n-1 left-tiles and at least n-1 right-tiles assumes that all tiles covering left region cells are left-tiles, i.e., they lie entirely in L. If there are mixed tiles, they could cover left region cells without being left-tiles. Then the lower bound that there are at least n-1 left-tiles would not hold, because some left region cells could be covered by mixed tiles. However, the argument that each L requires a tile with rightmost column L does not require that tile be a left-tile; it could be a mixed tile. So we still need at least n-1 tiles that have rightmost column L, but they could be mixed. Similarly, we need at least n-1 tiles that have leftmost column C. Could a single tile satisfy both a rightmost column L and a leftmost column C? Yes, if C ≤ L. So we could have a tile that counts for both a left requirement and a right requirement. So the lower bound of 2n-2 may be overcounting.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound may be invalid because it assumes disjointness of left and right tiles, which is not necessarily true. However, the construction achieving 2n-2 uses identity permutation where indeed any tile must be purely left or right, because for identity permutation, left region is left of diagonal, right region is right of diagonal, and any rectangle that includes both left and right cells would cross the diagonal and include a hole. So for identity permutation, left-tiles and right-tiles are indeed disjoint. So the lower bound holds for identity permutation. But the lower bound must hold for any permutation, because we are looking for minimal number of tiles across all possible permutations. The lower bound must be universal: for any placement of tiles (with some permutation of holes), we must have at least 2n-2 tiles. If we can find a placement with fewer tiles using a different permutation, then the lower bound fails. So we need to check if there exists any arrangement with fewer than 2n-2 tiles.\n\nLet's try to search for n=4 if we can achieve 5 tiles. We attempted one permutation and didn't find a 5 tile arrangement, but not exhaustive. Let's try to think more systematically.\n\nWe need to cover complement of a permutation matrix with rectangles. This is essentially the problem of partitioning the complement of a permutation matrix into rectangles that avoid the permutation points. The minimal number of rectangles needed is known as the \"rectangle covering number\" of the complement of a permutation matrix. I recall a known result: The complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into at most 2n-2 rectangles, and this bound is tight. Indeed, I think I've seen this problem before: The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all cells of an n×n board except a permutation of n cells (one per row and column) is exactly 2n-2. The construction uses the identity permutation and vertical strips. The lower bound is proven using the distinct row lengths argument and the fact that each rectangle can have at most one \"corner\" of the shape. Actually there is known result: The complement of a permutation matrix is a \"grid\" shape that can be partitioned into 2n-2 monotone rectangles, and this is optimal. The proof uses the fact that each rectangle can contain at most one cell from each \"staircase\" shape, leading to 2n-2 bound. Something like that.\n\nBut we need to verify if the lower bound proof given is correct. The solution's lower bound proof uses the distinct lengths argument, which is valid: For each L (distinct left region length), we need a tile whose rightmost column is L. However, does a mixed tile that also covers right region cells still have rightmost column L? Yes. So we still need at least n-1 tiles with distinct rightmost columns. Similarly, we need at least n-1 tiles with distinct leftmost columns. But a tile can have both a leftmost column and a rightmost column. So we need at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1 tiles? Actually we need at least n-1 tiles for rightmost columns and at least n-1 tiles for leftmost columns, but they could be the same tiles. So the lower bound is at least n-1, not necessarily 2n-2. However, we need to check if a tile can have both a distinct rightmost column and a distinct leftmost column that satisfy the required sets. Since we have n-1 distinct rightmost columns and n-1 distinct leftmost columns, each tile can provide at most one rightmost column and one leftmost column. So the minimal number of tiles needed to cover both sets is at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1. But we also need to cover all cells, not just the extreme cells. The extreme cells (rightmost left cells and leftmost right cells) are n-1 each. Covering those extreme cells ensures we have at least n-1 tiles for each side. But we also need to cover the interior cells of left and right regions. However, those interior cells can be covered by the same tiles that cover the extremes. So the lower bound might be n-1. But is there any other constraint that forces more tiles? Possibly the shape of left region (a Young diagram) cannot be covered by fewer than n-1 rectangles, even if rectangles can also cover right region cells. But the left region is a Young diagram shape with distinct row lengths. The known result: A Young diagram with distinct row lengths (i.e., a \"staircase\" shape) requires at least n-1 rectangles to partition (if rectangles must be axis-aligned and cannot cross the \"staircase\"? Actually the shape is the set of cells left of the permutation. For identity permutation, left region is a staircase shape (triangular). The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a staircase shape is indeed n-1 (vertical strips). But we need to cover both left and right region. However, each rectangle can only be placed in left region or right region, because if it crosses the diagonal (i.e., includes cells from both left and right region), it would contain a hole for some row (the diagonal cell). For identity permutation, crossing diagonal is impossible, as we argued. So each rectangle must be wholly left or right. So we need at least n-1 left rectangles + n-1 right rectangles = 2n-2. So for identity permutation, lower bound is 2n-2. But for other permutations, maybe we can reduce number of rectangles because the shape is not a perfect staircase; maybe we can have rectangles that cross the \"hole\" shape without covering holes. Let's explore.\n\nGeneral permutation shape: The set of holes is a permutation matrix. The complement consists of cells that are either left or right of each hole. The shape is not necessarily monotone; it's a union of two Young diagrams (left and right). The left region shape is a Young diagram defined by the permutation: for each row i, left region includes columns 1..p(i)-1. This shape is a \"Ferrers diagram\" determined by the permutation. The right region shape is the complement of the left region and the holes: columns p(i)+1..n. This shape is also a Young diagram after rotating or reflecting.\n\nNow, can a rectangle cover cells from both left and right region without covering a hole? For a given rectangle with rows a..b and columns c..d, we need that for each row i in [a,b], either p(i) < c (hole left of rectangle) or p(i) > d (hole right of rectangle). So the rectangle must be either entirely to the right of the hole for each row (i.e., c > p(i) for all rows) or entirely to the left (d < p(i) for all rows). So for each row, the hole must be on the same side of the rectangle (all left or all right). However, it's possible that for some rows the hole is left of rectangle, for others the hole is right of rectangle, as long as the rectangle does not intersect any hole. That means the rectangle's column interval must be a subset of the complement of the set {p(i) | i in [a,b]}. So the column interval must avoid all p(i) values for rows in the interval. So the column interval must be a contiguous interval that does not contain any of the p(i) values for those rows. So if the set of p(i) values for rows a..b is not contiguous (i.e., has gaps), we can place a rectangle in a gap. For example, suppose rows 1..3 have holes at columns 2,5,7. Then the column interval [3,4] avoids all holes, so rectangle can be placed there, covering cells left of some holes and right of others. So a rectangle can straddle across rows where holes are on different sides. So mixed tiles are possible.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 may not hold for arbitrary permutations. However, the problem asks for the minimum number of tiles needed, across all possible placements of tiles (including choice of permutation). So we can choose the permutation that yields the minimal number of tiles. The solution picks identity permutation and shows a construction achieving 2n-2. But perhaps there exists a permutation that allows fewer tiles. The lower bound must hold for any placement of tiles, not just for identity permutation. So we need to find a universal lower bound. The solution attempted to prove a universal lower bound of 2n-2 using the argument about distinct lengths. But we found a flaw: they assumed each tile lies entirely in L or R, which is false. However, they also used the distinct lengths argument to get at least n-1 left tiles and n-1 right tiles. That argument may still hold even if tiles can be mixed, because each tile can only have one rightmost column, but could also be counted as left tile? Let's examine the argument in detail:\n\nIn Section 2, they consider left region ℓ_i = p(i)-1. They argue that for each L (1..n-1), there is a unique row i_L with ℓ_i = L. They consider any rectangle tile that covers cell (i_L, L). They argue that the tile's rightmost column must be exactly L, because if it were larger, it would include the hole. So each L requires a tile whose rightmost column is L. Then they claim \"A tile has a single rightmost column, so a single tile can serve at most one value of L. Therefore at least n-1 tiles are necessary to cover all rows of ℒ.\" This argument does not require that tile be wholly left; it only uses that tile covering (i_L, L) must have rightmost column L. So indeed we need at least n-1 tiles that have rightmost column L for each L. However, a tile could also be used to cover some right region cells, but that does not affect the count of tiles needed for left region: we still need at least n-1 tiles (maybe some of those tiles also cover right region cells). So the lower bound for left region is n-1 tiles (some of which may also cover right region). Similarly, for right region, they argue that we need at least n-1 tiles with leftmost column C for each C. So we need at least n-1 tiles with distinct leftmost columns. A tile can have both a distinct rightmost column and a distinct leftmost column. So the total number of tiles needed is at least the size of a set of tiles that can simultaneously satisfy both sets of constraints. The minimal number of tiles needed to have n-1 distinct rightmost columns and n-1 distinct leftmost columns is at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1. However, we also need to ensure that each tile's leftmost column ≤ rightmost column. So we need to find a set of tiles with pairs (c,d) such that the set of d values includes all L=1..n-1, the set of c values includes all C=2..n, and c ≤ d for each tile. This is a matching problem as we discussed. The minimal number of tiles needed to satisfy both sets is at least n (since C=n cannot be paired with any L). So the lower bound is at least n. But the solution claims 2n-2. So the lower bound may be too high.\n\nBut perhaps there is an additional constraint that each tile can only cover cells from either left or right region, not both. If that holds for any permutation, then the lower bound 2n-2 would be correct. But we have found a counterexample where a tile can be mixed for a permutation where p(i) values are not monotonic. Let's construct a concrete small example where we can cover complement with fewer than 2n-2 tiles. Let's try n=4 with a permutation that yields a shape where we can use mixed tiles.\n\nWe need to find a covering of complement of permutation matrix with 5 rectangles. Let's try to find a permutation p = (2,4,1,3). So holes at (1,2),(2,4),(3,1),(4,3). Let's draw grid:\n\nRows 1..4, columns 1..4.\n\nMark holes:\n\n- Row1: hole at col2.\n- Row2: hole at col4.\n- Row3: hole at col1.\n- Row4: hole at col3.\n\nNow left region cells: for each row i, columns < p(i):\n\nRow1: col1 (since p(1)=2) -> left region cell (1,1)\nRow2: cols 1..3 (p(2)=4) -> (2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\nRow3: none (p(3)=1) -> left region empty\nRow4: cols 1..2 (p(4)=3) -> (4,1),(4,2)\n\nRight region cells: columns > p(i):\n\nRow1: cols 3..4 -> (1,3),(1,4)\nRow2: none (p(2)=4) -> none\nRow3: cols 2..4 -> (3,2),(3,3),(3,4)\nRow4: col4 -> (4,4)\n\nSo total cells to cover: left region cells: (1,1),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(4,1),(4,2) (6 cells). Right region cells: (1,3),(1,4),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4),(4,4) (6 cells). Total 12 cells.\n\nGoal: cover with 5 rectangles.\n\nWe need to find rectangles that avoid holes.\n\nLet's list holes again: (1,2),(2,4),(3,1),(4,3).\n\nNow consider potential rectangles.\n\nWe can try vertical rectangle covering column 1 rows 1-4? Column 1 includes hole (3,1) which is a hole, not allowed. So can't.\n\nColumn 1 rows 1-2? Contains (1,1) and (2,1) which are left region cells, no hole (hole at (3,1) not in rows 1-2). So tile covering (1,1),(2,1) is allowed. That's vertical tile col1 rows1-2.\n\nColumn 1 rows 4 only? (4,1) left region cell, allowed. Could combine (4,1) with (4,2) maybe.\n\nColumn 2 rows 2-4? Column2 includes (2,2) left region, (3,2) right region, (4,2) left region. No hole at column2? Holes are at (1,2) row1 col2, (2,4), (3,1), (4,3). So column2 rows2-4 does not include any hole. So tile covering column2 rows2-4 is allowed. That tile would cover (2,2),(3,2),(4,2). Good.\n\nColumn 3 rows 1-3? Column3 includes (1,3) right region, (2,3) left region, (3,3) right region. No hole at column3? Holes: (4,3) row4 col3, not in rows1-3. So tile covering column3 rows1-3 is allowed. That tile would cover (1,3),(2,3),(3,3). Good.\n\nColumn4 rows1-3? Column4 includes (1,4) right region, (2,4) hole (hole at (2,4)), (3,4) right region. So can't include row2.\n\nColumn4 rows1-1? (1,4) alone okay. Column4 rows3-4? (3,4) right region, (4,4) right region, no hole (hole at (2,4) not in rows3-4). So tile covering column4 rows3-4 is allowed. That tile would cover (3,4),(4,4).\n\nNow let's see which cells are covered:\n\nTile A: col1 rows1-2 covers (1,1),(2,1).\nTile B: col2 rows2-4 covers (2,2),(3,2),(4,2).\nTile C: col3 rows1-3 covers (1,3),(2,3),(3,3).\nTile D: col4 rows3-4 covers (3,4),(4,4).\n\nRemaining cells: (4,1) (left region), (1,4) (right region). Also (2,???) Actually we covered (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) left region cells. (2,1) left, (2,2) left, (2,3) left. Good. (4,1) left not covered. (1,4) right not covered. Also (???) Let's list all cells we needed to cover:\n\nLeft region: (1,1) covered by A, (2,1) covered by A, (2,2) covered by B, (2,3) covered by C, (4,1) not covered, (4,2) covered by B.\n\nRight region: (1,3) covered by C, (1,4) not covered, (3,2) covered by B, (3,3) covered by C, (3,4) covered by D, (4,4) covered by D.\n\nSo uncovered cells: (4,1) and (1,4). These are two cells not covered. We can cover them with a single tile? They are not aligned (different rows and columns). Could we cover them with a rectangle? The rectangle covering rows 1-4, columns 1-4 includes holes, not allowed. Could we cover them with a rectangle covering rows 1-4, column 1? That includes (3,1) hole, not allowed. Could we cover them with rectangle covering rows 1-4, column 4? That includes (2,4) hole, not allowed. Could we cover them with rectangle covering rows 1-4, columns 1-4 but excluding holes? Not allowed. So we need separate tiles for each: tile E covering (4,1) alone, tile F covering (1,4) alone. That yields total tiles = 6 (A,B,C,D,E,F). That's 6, which equals 2n-2 = 6. So we didn't achieve 5.\n\nBut maybe we can adjust tile selection to reduce to 5. Let's try to combine (4,1) with something else. Could we extend tile A to include row4? Column1 rows1-4 includes hole (3,1), not allowed. Could we combine (1,4) with something else? Could we extend tile D to include row1? Column4 rows1-4 includes hole (2,4), not allowed. Could we use a horizontal tile covering (4,1) and (4,2)? Row4 columns1-2 includes (4,1) left region, (4,2) left region, and hole at (4,3) not in columns1-2, so okay. So we can replace tile A (col1 rows1-2) and tile B (col2 rows2-4) with a horizontal tile covering row4 columns1-2 (covers (4,1),(4,2)). But we also need to cover (1,1) and (2,1) and (2,2). Let's see.\n\nAlternative approach: Use tile covering row1 columns1-1 (just (1,1)). Use tile covering row2 columns1-2 (covers (2,1),(2,2)). Use tile covering column3 rows1-3 (covers (1,3),(2,3),(3,3)). Use tile covering column2 rows3-4 (covers (3,2),(4,2)). Use tile covering column4 rows3-4 (covers (3,4),(4,4)). Then we still have (4,1) and (1,4) uncovered. So still need 2 more tiles.\n\nMaybe we can use a tile covering (1,1) and (1,4) together? That's not a rectangle (different columns). Could we use a tile covering rows1-4, columns1-4 but with a hole? Not allowed.\n\nThus for this permutation, we still need 6 tiles. Let's try a different permutation.\n\nPerhaps the minimal number of tiles is indeed 2n-2 for all permutations. Let's search for known results. I recall a known combinatorial geometry problem: \"Cover an n×n board with rectangles such that each row and column has exactly one uncovered cell.\" The answer is 2n-2. The solution uses the argument that each rectangle can have at most one \"corner\" of the uncovered cells, leading to 2n-2 bound. This is a known problem, I think from some contest. The solution given is typical: The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix; each rectangle can cover at most one cell from each row and column? Actually each rectangle can cover at most one \"hole\"? Let's recall known results: The complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into 2n-2 monotone rectangles; this is optimal. I think it's a known result: The minimal number of monotone rectangles needed to partition an n×n board with a permutation of n points removed is exactly 2n-2. This is known as \"rectangular covering of a permutation matrix complement\". The proof uses the distinct row lengths argument as given. The key is that each rectangle can have at most one \"rightmost left cell\" and at most one \"leftmost right cell\". So you need at least n-1 rectangles for each side. But they also argue that a rectangle cannot serve both a left and a right requirement because it would have to contain a hole. However, we argued that a rectangle could avoid holes if the holes are not in the column interval. But perhaps the argument that a rectangle cannot serve both a left and a right requirement holds because the left requirement cell (i_L, L) is the rightmost left cell in its row, and the right requirement cell (i_C, C) is the leftmost right cell in its row. For a rectangle to contain both cells, we need its column interval to include both column L and column C. Since L < C? Actually L is p(i_L)-1, C = p(i_C)+1. For identity permutation, L = i-1, C = i+1, so L < C. In general, we have L = p(i_L)-1, C = p(i_C)+1. Since p(i_L) = L+1, p(i_C) = C-1. So L = p(i_L)-1, C = p(i_C)+1. For a rectangle to contain both cells, we need its column interval [c,d] to satisfy c ≤ L ≤ d and c ≤ C ≤ d. So we need c ≤ min(L,C) and d ≥ max(L,C). So the interval must contain both L and C. Since L and C are distinct (except maybe when L+2 = C?), but anyway the interval must contain the whole range between them. That means the interval includes all columns between L and