                "content": "answer.\n\n**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{2025}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Reformulation:**  \n   The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: one empty unit square in each row and each column. After permuting rows and columns we may assume the empty squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) with \\(n=2025\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound (construction):**  \n   For each row \\(i\\) place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) covering all squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\).  \n   - The tiles are disjoint because they lie in different rows.  \n   - In row \\(i\\) the only uncovered square is \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - In column \\(j\\) the only uncovered square is \\((j,j)\\).  \n   Hence we obtain a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n3. **Lower bound (rank argument):**  \n   Any tiling corresponds to a partition of the off‑diagonal entries of the \\(n\\times n\\) matrix \\(J-I\\) (where \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix) into rectangles \\((I_k,J_k)\\) with \\(I_k\\cap J_k=\\varnothing\\).  \n   - Define binary matrices \\(A\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times t}\\) and \\(B\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times t}\\) by \\(A_{i,k}=1\\iff i\\in I_k\\) and \\(B_{j,k}=1\\iff j\\in J_k\\).  \n   - Then \\(\\displaystyle AB^{\\mathsf T}=J-I\\).  \n   - Over the reals, \\(\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n\\) (its eigenvalues are \\(n-1\\) and \\(-1\\) with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  \n   - Since \\(\\operatorname{rank}(AB^{\\mathsf T})\\le\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le t\\), we obtain \\(t\\ge n\\).  \n\n4. **Conclusion:**  \n   The construction uses \\(n\\) tiles and the lower bound shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles. Therefore the minimum number of tiles is \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA unit square of the grid will be denoted by its row and column indices \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reformulation\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nPermuting rows according to \\(\\pi^{-1}\\) and columns according to the identity sends \\(U\\) to the main diagonal  \n\\(\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nRow‑ and column‑permutations do not change the number of tiles needed, so we may (and shall) assume\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with \\(n\\) tiles (upper bound)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\},\n\\qquad\\text{size }1\\times (n-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe rectangles \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_n\\) are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct rows.  \nEvery square not belonging to \\(U\\) lies in exactly one of the \\(R_i\\); the only square of row \\(i\\) not covered by \\(R_i\\) is \\((i,i)\\).  \n\nConsequently:\n\n* each row \\(i\\) contains exactly one uncovered square, namely \\((i,i)\\);\n* each column \\(j\\) contains exactly one uncovered square, namely \\((j,j)\\).\n\nThus the family \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\) is a valid tiling using **\\(n\\) tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Any tiling needs at least \\(n\\) tiles (lower bound)\n\nLet a tiling satisfying the condition be given.  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{R}=\\{(I_k,J_k)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,t\\}\n\\]\n\nthe collection of its tiles, where \\(I_k\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the set of rows covered by the \\(k\\)-th tile and \\(J_k\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the set of columns it covers.  \nBecause a tile cannot contain an uncovered square, we have  \n\n\\[\nI_k\\cap J_k=\\varnothing\\qquad\\text{for every }k. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, by (1) each off‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs to exactly one tile; equivalently\n\n\\[\n\\forall\\,i\\neq j\\;:\\;\\exists!\\,k\\text{ such that }i\\in I_k\\text{ and }j\\in J_k.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDefine binary matrices \\(A,B\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\,n\\times t}\\) by  \n\n\\[\nA_{i,k}= \\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if }i\\in I_k,\\\\[2pt]\n0 & \\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nB_{j,k}= \\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if }j\\in J_k,\\\\[2pt]\n0 & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor any pair \\((i,j)\\),\n\n\\[\n(AB^{\\mathsf T})_{i,j}\n   =\\sum_{k=1}^{t}A_{i,k}B_{j,k}\n   =\\#\\{k\\mid i\\in I_k,\\;j\\in J_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy (4) this number equals \\(1\\) when \\(i\\neq j\\) and equals \\(0\\) when \\(i=j\\) (the latter follows from (3), because a tile never contains a pair \\((i,i)\\)).  \nHence\n\n\\[\nAB^{\\mathsf T}=J-I,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) denotes the \\(n\\times n\\) all‑ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity matrix.\n\nThe matrix \\(J-I\\) has eigenvalues \\(n-1\\) (once) and \\(-1\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\operatorname{rank}(AB^{\\mathsf T})\\le\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le t\\), (6) and (7) give  \n\n\\[\nt\\;\\ge\\;\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)\\;=\\;n.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** tiling satisfying the requirement uses at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimal number of tiles\n\nThe construction of Section 2 uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles, and the lower bound of Section 3 shows that fewer than \\(n\\) tiles are impossible. Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(n\\).\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\), so the answer is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
