\n   Hence for \\(c=2,\\dots ,n-1\\) we have \\(r_c+s_c\\ge2\\), while for the extreme columns \\(c=1\\) and \\(c=n\\) we have \\(r_1+s_1\\ge1\\) and \\(r_n+s_n\\ge1\\).  \n   Summing gives\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=1}^{n}(r_c+s_c)\\ge (n-2)\\cdot2+1+1=2n-2.\n   \\]\n   Therefore **any** tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound:**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\).  \n   - The left part \\(L\\) is the strict lower triangle \\(\\{(i,j):j<i\\}\\).  \n     For each column \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     T_c^{L}=\\{(i,c):i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     which is a \\((n-c)\\times1\\) tile.  \n   - The right part \\(R\\) is the strict upper triangle \\(\\{(i,j):j>i\\}\\).  \n     For each column \\(c=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     T_c^{R}=\\{(i,c):i=1,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\((c-1)\\times1\\) tile.  \n\n   The two families are disjoint, cover all cells of \\(L\\cup R\\), and consist of exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n6. **Conclusion:**  \n   The lower bound and the construction coincide, so the minimal possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with an \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares whose rows are numbered \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and columns \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the unit square whose column is \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nFor a row \\(i\\) the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) splits the row into two (possibly empty) contiguous horizontal segments:\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j):\\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\nClearly \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\) and \\(L\\cup R\\) is exactly the set of all unit squares that are *covered* by tiles.\n\nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; therefore a tile cannot contain a point of \\(U\\). Consequently **every tile lies entirely inside \\(L\\) or entirely inside \\(R\\).**  \nHence the total number of tiles equals the number of tiles used for \\(L\\) plus the number used for \\(R\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Column‑wise interval decomposition  \n\nFix a column \\(c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)).  \nDefine the row sets\n\\[\nA_c=\\{i\\mid \\pi(i)>c\\},\\qquad B_c=\\{i\\mid \\pi(i)<c\\}.\n\\]\nThe cells of \\(L\\) that lie in column \\(c\\) are precisely\n\\[\nL\\cap\\{(i,c):1\\le i\\le n\\}= \\{(i,c):i\\in A_c\\},\n\\]\nand the cells of \\(R\\) in column \\(c\\) are\n\\[\nR\\cap\\{(i,c):1\\le i\\le n\\}= \\{(i,c):i\\in B_c\\}.\n\\]\n\nA tile that lies in \\(L\\) and uses column \\(c\\) must have its row interval contained in \\(A_c\\); because a tile’s rows must be contiguous, one such tile can cover at most **one maximal contiguous interval** of \\(A_c\\).  \nLet \\(r_c\\) be the number of maximal contiguous intervals of \\(A_c\\).  \nThen at least \\(r_c\\) tiles are necessary to cover all cells of \\(L\\) in column \\(c\\).\n\nAnalogously, let \\(s_c\\) be the number of maximal contiguous intervals of \\(B_c\\).  \nAt least \\(s_c\\) tiles are necessary to cover all cells of \\(R\\) in column \\(c\\).\n\nSumming over all columns we obtain the fundamental lower bound\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(r_c+s_c)}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  A universal lower bound for \\(\\sum(r_c+s_c)\\)\n\nFor each column \\(c\\) there is a unique row \\(i_c\\) with \\(\\pi(i_c)=c\\) (the row containing the uncovered square of column \\(c\\)).  \n\n*If \\(c<n\\):*  \nThere are \\(n-c\\ge1\\) columns larger than \\(c\\); consequently at least one row satisfies \\(\\pi(i)>c\\). Hence \\(A_c\\neq\\varnothing\\) and therefore \\(r_c\\ge1\\).\n\n*If \\(c>1\\):*  \nThere are \\(c-1\\ge1\\) columns smaller than \\(c\\); consequently at least one row satisfies \\(\\pi(i)<c\\). Hence \\(B_c\\neq\\varnothing\\) and therefore \\(s_c\\ge1\\).\n\nThus we have\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&c=1   &&\\Rightarrow\\; r_1\\ge1,\\; s_1=0,\\\\\n&c=n   &&\\Rightarrow\\; r_n=0,\\; s_n\\ge1,\\\\\n&2\\le c\\le n-1 &&\\Rightarrow\\; r_c\\ge1,\\; s_c\\ge1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nConsequently,\n\\[\nr_c+s_c\\ge\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }c=1\\text{ or }c=n,\\\\[2pt]\n2 &\\text{if }2\\le c\\le n-1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSumming these inequalities yields\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{n}(r_c+s_c)\\;\\ge\\;(n-2)\\cdot2+1+1\\;=\\;2n-2.\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (2) we obtain the **general lower bound**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Any tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n- **Left region \\(L\\).**  \n  Here \\(L=\\{(i,j):j<i\\}\\) (the strict lower triangle).  \n  For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define the vertical rectangle\n  \\[\n  T_c^{L}= \\{(i,c): i=c+1,c+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n  \\]\n  This rectangle has height \\(n-c\\) and width \\(1\\); its rows form a single contiguous interval, so it is a valid tile.  \n  The family \\(\\{T_c^{L}\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) covers every cell of \\(L\\) exactly once.\n\n- **Right region \\(R\\).**  \n  Here \\(R=\\{(i,j):j>i\\}\\) (the strict upper triangle).  \n  For each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define the vertical rectangle\n  \\[\n  T_c^{R}= \\{(i,c): i=1,2,\\d