d{cases}\n\\]\nThe entry \\(M_{ij}=1\\) means that the square \\((i,j)\\) is covered by a tile, and \\(M_{ij}=0\\) means it is uncovered.  \nHence \\(M=J-P\\), where \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix and \\(P\\) is the permutation matrix of the identity (the diagonal).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Rectangles as rank‑1 matrices  \n\nA rectangular tile covering the rows \\(r_1,\\dots ,r_2\\) and the columns \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_2\\) corresponds to the matrix\n\\[\nR=\\mathbf{u}\\mathbf{v}^{\\!T},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{u}_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&r_1\\le i\\le r_2,\\\\\n0,&\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{v}_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&c_1\\le j\\le c_2,\\\\\n0,&\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThus every tile contributes a rank‑1 matrix, and a tiling of the board corresponds to a decomposition of \\(M\\) into a sum of rank‑1 matrices whose supports are pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  No tile may contain a diagonal cell  \n\nSince every diagonal entry of \\(M\\) equals \\(0\\), a tile (which would contribute a \\(1\\) on all positions of its support) cannot contain any diagonal cell.  \nConsequently each tile lies **entirely** in the set  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad L=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\},\n\\]\nthe strict upper‑ and lower‑triangular parts of the board.  \nHence the decomposition of \\(M\\) splits into two independent decompositions:\n\\[\nM=U+L,\\qquad\nU_{ij}=1\\;(i<j),\\;0\\text{ otherwise},\\qquad\nL_{ij}=1\\;(i>j),\\;0\\text{ otherwise}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Rank of the upper‑ (and lower‑) triangular matrix  \n\nConsider the matrix \\(U\\). For a column vector \\(x=(x_1,\\dots ,x_n)^{\\!T}\\) we have\n\\[\n(Ux)_i=\\sum_{j=i+1}^{n}x_j .\n\\]\nIf \\(Ux=0\\), then in particular \\((Ux)_{n-1}=x_n=0\\); then \\((Ux)_{n-2}=x_{n-1}=0\\), and proceeding up