imes(n-i)\\) covering the cells \\((i,i+1),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) (the right part).  \n   These tiles are pairwise disjoint, cover every non‑diagonal cell, and there are exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) of them.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable, so it is the minimum.  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) the minimum number of tiles is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the condition leaves exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation  \n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nFor a fixed permutation \\(p\\) define the two subsets of covered squares  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus every covered square belongs either to \\(L\\) (the *left* of its row’s hole) or to \\(R\\) (the *right* of its row’s hole).  \nA rectangle that contains a square of row \\(i\\) cannot intersect the hole \\((i,p(i))\\); therefore the whole rectangle must lie completely in \\(L\\) or completely in \\(R\\) for that row.  \nConsequently a tiling of the board induces a partition of \\(L\\) into axis‑parallel rectangles and a partition of \\(R\\) into axis‑parallel rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (left part needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) the cell \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) and is the **rightmost** cell of row \\(i\\) inside \\(L\\).  \nIf a rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq L\\) contains \\((i,p(i)-1)\\), then the rightmost column of \\(Q\\) must be exactly \\(p(i)-1\\); otherwise either\n\n* the rectangle would extend to a column \\(\\ge p(i)\\), which does not belong to \\(L\\), or