 p(i)-1, because if r_i > p(i)-1, then rectangle would include column p(i) and thus cover the hole, contradiction. So each rectangle covering left part of row i cannot extend beyond column p(i)-1. Since each rectangle has a single rightmost column, it can serve at most one row i. So we need at least as many rectangles as rows with p(i)>1, i.e., n-1. This seems correct. Similarly for right part. So lower bound 2n-2 is correct.\n\nNow is there any possibility of using rectangles that cover both left and right parts of a row? The argument says a rectangle cannot intersect both sides of the hole in the same row because it would have to contain the hole. Indeed, any rectangle covering squares left of the hole and squares right of the hole would have to include the column of the hole (since rectangle includes all columns between its leftmost and rightmost). So it would cover the hole, which is not allowed. So each rectangle can only be wholly left or wholly right of the hole for each row. However, a rectangle could span multiple rows. The argument for left part uses each rectangle has a single rightmost column. But a rectangle covering left part of multiple rows could have the same rightmost column for all those rows? Let's examine: Suppose we have rows i and i' where p(i)>1 and p(i')>1. Could a single rectangle cover c_i and c_i'? The rectangle would have to include columns up to max(p(i)-1, p(i')-1). But the rectangle's rightmost column is the same for all rows it covers. If p(i)-1 != p(i')-1, then the rectangle's rightmost column would be the larger of the two, but then for the row with smaller p(i)-1, the rectangle would also include column p(i) (the hole) because its rightmost column > p(i)-1, which would include column p(i). But does the rectangle necessarily include column p(i)? Let's think: Suppose we have a rectangle covering rows i and i' and columns from some leftmost column L to some rightmost column R. For row i, the hole is at column p(i). If R >= p(i), then