i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  In this case\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\n*For the left side.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_{j}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_{j}\\) is a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\); it contains no diagonal cell.  \nThe family \\(\\{V_{j}\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles and covers every cell of \\(L\\).\n\n*For the right side.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_{i}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_{i}\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\); it also avoids the diagonal.  \nThe family \\(\\{H_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles and covers every cell of \\(R\\).\n\nAltogether we have exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\naxis\u2011parallel rectangles, they are disjoint, they avoid all uncovered squares, and they cover every other unit square of the board.  Hence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
