g applied to the region \\(R\\) (using the leftmost cells of each row) yields:\n\n#### Lemma 4 (Lower bound for \\(R\\)).  \nAny tiling of \\(R\\) by rectangles needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.* In \\(R\\) the leftmost cell of row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i)+1)\\); the column indices \\(\\pi(i)+1\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). The argument of Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 with \u201crightmost\u2019\u2019 replaced by \u201cleftmost\u2019\u2019 gives the claim. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary (Global lower bound).  \nEvery tiling of the whole board (with the required holes) uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound.  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \nThen \\(\\ell_i=i-1\\) and \\(r_i=n-i\\).\n\n*Tiles covering \\(L\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{L}:=\\{(i,c):c+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_c^{L}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\); it lies entirely in \\(L\\) because for every row \\(i\\ge c+1\\) we have \\(\\ell_i=i-1\\ge c\\). The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_c^{L}\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(L\\).\n\n*Tiles covering \\(R\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{R}:=\\{(i,c):1\\le i\\le c-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_c^{R}\\) has size \\((c-1)\\times1\\) and lies in \\(R\\) because for every row \\(i\\le c-1\\) we have \\(r_i=n-i\\ge n-(c-1)=c\\!-\\!1\\), i.e. column \\(c\\) is to the right of the hole. The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_c^{R}\\}_{c=2}^{n}\\) are disjoint from each other and from the \\(T_c^{L}\\), and they cover all cells of \\(R\\).\n\nThus the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\n\\[\n\\{T_c^{L}\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{T_c^{R}\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nform 