ach row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square,\nthere is a bijection \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that\nthe uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\nThus the set of uncovered squares is\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Every tile lies entirely on one side of the permutation  \n\nFor a rectangle \\(T\\) let  \n\\[\nI_T=[a,b]\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\nJ_T=[c,d]\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nbe the (contiguous) sets of rows and columns it occupies.\nIf \\((i,j)\\in T\\) then \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le j\\le d\\).\n\nFix a row \\(i\\in I_T\\).  \nBecause \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\notin T\\), either \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\) (the whole\nrectangle lies left of the hole in that row) or \\(c>\\sigma(i)\\) (the\nrectangle lies right of the hole).\n\nSuppose there exist rows \\(i,i'\\in I_T\\) with\n\\(d<\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(c>\\sigma(i')\\).\nThen\n\\[\n\\sigma(i')<c\\le d<\\sigma(i).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nFor any row \\(r\\in I_T\\) the column \\(\\sigma(r)\\) cannot belong to the\ninterval \\([c,d]\\), otherwise \\((r,\\sigma(r))\\) would be a hole inside\n\\(T\\).  Hence each \\(\\sigma(r)\\) is either \\(\\le\\sigma(i')\\) or\n\\(\\ge\\sigma(i)\\).  Because the rows in \\(I_T\\) are consecutive,\n(1) forces the existence of a row \\(r\\) with\n\\(\\sigma(i')<\\sigma(r)<\\sigma(i)\\), a contradiction.\nTherefore such a pair \\(i,i'\\) cannot exist, and all rows of \\(I_T\\) satisfy\nthe same inequality.  Consequently\n\n* either \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\) for every \\(i\\in I_T\\) – the rectangle lies\n  entirely in the **left region**\n  \\[\n  L:=\\{(i,j):j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n  \\]\n* or \\(c>\\sigma(i)\\) for every \\(i\\in I_T\\) – the rectangle lies\n  entirely in the **right region**\n  \\[\n  R:=\\{(i,j):j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n  \\]\n\nThus every tile is wholly contained in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\).\n\n-----------