. \u220e\n\n*Claim.*  No rectangle can contain two distinct cells \\(a_i\\) and\n\\(a_{i'}\\) with \\(i\\neq i'\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose without loss of generality \\(p(i)<p(i')\\).  \nIf a rectangle contained both \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_{i'}\\), its column interval\nwould have to include column \\(p(i)\\) (because it contains\n\\(p(i)-1\\) and is contiguous).  Column \\(p(i)\\) is precisely the hole\n\\((i,p(i))\\), contradicting the requirement that a tile contain no\nhole. \u220e\n\nThus each rectangle covering \\(L\\) can contain at most one cell \\(a_i\\).\nExactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=1\\); all other \\(N-1\\) rows satisfy\n\\(p(i)>1\\) and therefore produce a distinct cell \\(a_i\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling of }L\\text{ uses at least }N-1\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for covering \\(R\\)\n\nThe argument is symmetric.  For each row with \\(p(i)<N\\) define  \n\n\\[\nb_i=(i,p(i)+1)\\in R .\n\\]\n\nExactly one row has \\(p(i)=N\\); the remaining \\(N-1\\) rows give distinct\ncells \\(b_i\\).  By the same reasoning as above, each rectangle that lies\nin \\(R\\) can contain at most one such cell, and each must be the\nleftmost cell of its rectangle.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling of }R\\text{ uses at least }N-1\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Combined lower bound\n\nSince the tiles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\) are disjoint,\n(1) and (2) together imply that any tiling of the whole board needs at\nleast  \n\n\\[\n(N-1)+(N-1)=2(N-1)\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le N),\n\\]\n\nso the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for all \\(i\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) we place\n\n* a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times (i-1)\\) covering the cells\n  \\((i,1),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\) (the left part of the row);\n* a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times (N-i)\\) covering the cells\n  \\((i,i+1),\\dots ,(i,N)\\) (the right part of the row).\n\nFor \\(i=1\\) the left part is empty, so only the right tile is placed;\nfor \\(i=N\\) the right part is empty, so only the left tile is placed.\nAll tiles are axis\u2011aligned, lie completely on one side of the diagonal\nhole in their row, and no two tiles overlap.  Every unit square that is\nnot a diagonal hole belongs to exactly one of the placed tiles.\n\nThe number of tiles used is\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(N-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\n\\underbrace{(N-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}=2(N-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound \\(2(N-1)\\) and the explicit construction both give the\nsame number of tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2(N-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
