t of each uncovered square in each row. For each row i with p(i)>1, the leftmost cell in that row that is in L is column 1, and the rightmost cell in L in that row is column p(i)-1. So the cell a_i is the rightmost cell in L in row i. So any rectangle that lies entirely in L and includes any cell from row i must have its rightmost column ≤ p(i)-1. If the rectangle includes any cell from row i, then its rightmost column is at most p(i)-1. But does it need to contain a_i? Not necessarily; it could be a rectangle that lies entirely in L but does not include the rightmost column of that row. For instance, consider row i where p(i)=5. L includes columns 1,2,3,4. The rectangle could be a 2x2 rectangle covering columns 1-2 and rows i and i+1, not including column 4 (the a_i). So that rectangle does not contain a_i. So the argument that each rectangle must contain at least one a_i is not explicitly stated. However, they argue that any covering of L by rectangles requires at least N-1 rectangles. They rely on the fact that each rectangle can contain at most one a_i, but they also need to argue that each rectangle must contain at least one a_i, otherwise you could have more rectangles than needed? Actually they need a lower bound: number of rectangles needed to cover L is at least N-1. If each rectangle can contain at most one a_i, then the number of rectangles covering L must be at least the number of a_i that need to be covered. But do we need to cover a_i? Yes, because a_i are cells in L. So any covering of L must cover all cells in L, including a_i. So each rectangle can cover at most one a_i, and there are N-1 a_i cells. So we need at least N-1 rectangles to cover all a_i. This is valid: each rectangle can cover at most one a_i, but we need to cover all a_i. So we need at least N-1 rectangles. This is a correct lower bound.\n\nSimilarly for R. So total tiles >= 2(N-1). This is correct.\n\nNow the construction: identity permutation p(i)=i. Then L consists of cells left of