\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]  \nwhere the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\},\n\\]  \n\nthe left and right parts of the row that must be covered.  \nThe set of all squares that have to be tiled is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(L_i\\cup R_i\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nA **tile** is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior consists only of squares from \\(\\mathcal C\\); tiles are required to be pairwise disjoint and their union must equal \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nLet \\(\\sigma=\\pi^{-1}\\); thus \\(\\sigma(j)\\) is the row that contains the uncovered square in column \\(j\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_L=\\{(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\},\\qquad   \nS_U=\\{(\\sigma(j)-1,j)\\mid \\sigma(j)>1\\},\n\\]  \n\nand let \\(S=S_L\\cup S_U\\).\n\n*Counting the points of \\(S\\).*  \nExactly one row can have \\(\\pi(i)=1\\); all other \\(n-1\\) rows satisfy \\(\\pi(i)>1\\), hence \\(|S_L|=n-1\\).  \nExactly one column can have \\(\\sigma(j)=1\\); all other \\(n-1\\) columns satisfy \\(\\sigma(j)>1\\), hence \\(|S_U|=n-1\\).  \nThe two sets are disjoint (a point cannot be simultaneously left‑adjacent to a hole and above‑adjacent to a (possibly different) hole), therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S|=|S_L|+|S_U|=2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Each tile contains at most one point of \\(S\\).*  \n\nLet \\(R\\) be any rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares.  \nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two distinct points of \\(S\\).  \nThree cases are possible.\n\n1. **Both points belong to \\(S_L\\).**  \n   Let the points be \\((i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\((i',\\pi(i')-1)\\) with \\(i\\neq i'\\).  \n   Without loss assume \\(\\pi(i)<\\pi(i')\\).  \n   The column interval of \\(R\\) contains both columns \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) and \\(\\pi(i')-1\\); consequently it also contains the column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \n   Since row \\(i\\) lies in the row interval of \\(R\\), the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) (an uncovered square) belongs to \\(R\\), a contradiction.\n\n2. **Both points belong to \\(S_U\\).**  \n   Symmetrically, if \\((\\sigma(j)-1,j)\\) and \\((\\sigma(j')-1,j')\\) are in \\(R\\) with \\(j<j'\\), then the row interval of \\(R\\) contains the row \\(\\sigma(j)\\), and the column interval contains column \\(j\\); thus \\((\\sigma(j),j)\\) (an uncovered square) lies in \\(R\\), again a contradiction.\n\n3. **One point from \\(S_L\\) and one from \\(S_U\\).**  \n   Suppose \\((i,\\pi(i)-1)\\in S_L\\) and \\((\\sigma(j)-1,j)\\in S_U\\) are both in \\(R\\).  \n   The row interval of \\(R\\) contains rows \\(i\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)-1\\); hence it also contains the intermediate row \\(\\sigma(j)\\).  \n   The column interval contains columns \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) and \\(j\\); therefore it also contains the intermediate column \\(j\\).  \n   Consequently the cell \\((\\sigma(j),j)\\) (an uncovered square) belongs to \\(R\\), a contradiction.\n\nThus **no rectangle can contain two distinct points of \\(S\\)**.  \nSince each tile is a rectangle, each tile covers at most one element of \\(S\\).  \nBy (1) at least \\(|S|=2n-2\\) tiles are required:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Number of tiles}\\ge 2n-2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An upper bound – a construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nHence the uncovered squares are precisely the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{L}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_j^{\\text{L}}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\) lying strictly **below** the diagonal; it avoids all uncovered squares because every row \\(i>j\\) has its hole at column \\(i>j\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le