ghtarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\nDefine the binary matrix \\(A=(a_{i,j})_{1\\le i,j\\le n}\\) by  \n\n\\[\na_{i,j}=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&j=\\pi(i)\\quad(\\text{hole}),\\\\[2pt]\n1,&j\\neq\\pi(i)\\quad(\\text{cell that must be tiled}).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nA **tile** is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose interior consists only of cells with entry \\(1\\); in matrix language a tile is a submatrix of \\(A\\) that is all\u2011\\(1\\).  \nThe tiles must be pairwise disjoint and their union must be exactly the set of all \\(1\\)-entries of \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consider the two positions  \n\n\\[\np_i=(i,\\pi(i+1)),\\qquad q_i=(i+1,\\pi(i)).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, \\(\\pi(i)\\neq\\pi(i+1)\\); consequently both \\(p_i\\) and \\(q_i\\) satisfy \\(j\\neq\\pi(i)\\) for their respective rows, i.e. they are \\(1\\)-entries of \\(A\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nF=\\{p_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{q_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|F|=2(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1  \nNo axis\u2011parallel rectangle consisting only of \\(1\\)-entries of \\(A\\) can contain two distinct elements of \\(F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a rectangle \\(R\\) be defined by a consecutive set of rows \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and a consecutive set of columns \\([c_1,c_2]\\) (with \\(r_1\\le r_2,\\;c_1\\le c_2\\)).  \nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two distinct points of \\(F\\). Three possibilities arise.\n\n1. **Both points are of the form \\(p_i\\).**  \n   Suppose \\(p_i=(i,\\pi(i+1))\\) and \\(p_j=(j,\\pi(j+1))\\) belong to \\(R\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   Then the row interval of \\(R\\) contains the rows \\(i,i+1,\\dots ,j\\); in particular it contains row \\(i+1\\).  \n   The column interval contains the columns \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) and \\(\\pi(j+1)\\); hence it contains column \\(\\pi(i+1)\\).  \n   The entry \\((i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\) is a hole (its value in \\(A\\) is \\(0\\)), and it lies inside \\(R\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(R\\) consists only of \\(1\\)\u2019s.\n\n2. **Both points are of the form \\(q_i\\).**  \n   This case is symmetric to the previous one (swap rows and columns).  \n   If \\(q_i=(i+1,\\pi(i))\\) and \\(q_j=(j+1,\\pi(j))\\) with \\(i<j\\) lie in \\(R\\), then the column interval contains column \\(\\pi(i)\\) and the row interval contains row \\(i+1\\); consequently the hole \\((i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\) (which equals \\((i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\) because \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) is the hole column of row \\(i+1\\)) lies in \\(R\\), a contradiction.\n\n3. **One point of each type.**  \n   Suppose \\(p_i=(i,\\pi(i+1))\\) and \\(q_j=(j+1,\\pi(j))\\) belong to \\(R\\).  \n   Without loss of generality assume \\(i\\le j\\).  \n   Then the row interval of \\(R\\) contains rows \\(i,\\dots ,j+1\\), hence it contains row \\(i+1\\).  \n   The column interval contains columns \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) and \\(\\pi(j)\\); therefore it contains column \\(\\pi(i+1)\\).  \n   Again the hole \\((i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\) lies inside \\(R\\), contradicting the definition of a tile.\n\nIn every case a rectangle containing two distinct elements of \\(F\\) would also contain a hole, which is impossible. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 each tile can contain at most one element of the set \\(F\\).  \nSince every element of \\(F\\) must be covered (they are \\(1\\)-entries of \\(A\\)), the number of tiles is at least \\(|F|=2n-2\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling requires at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe holes are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{below}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_j^{\\text{below}}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\); it lies strictly **below** the diagonal, therefore it contains no hole.\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{above}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_j^{\\text{above}}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\); it lies strictly **above** the diagonal and also contains no hole.\n\nThe two families \\(\\{T_j^{\\text{below}}\\}_{j=1}^{\\,n-1}\\) and \\(\\{T_j^{\\text{above}}\\}_{j=2}^{\\,n}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy different columns, and within a fixed column the \u201cabove\u2019\u2019 and \u201cbelow\u2019\u2019 tiles occupy disjoint rows.  \nTheir union is exactly the set of all cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), i.e. all \\(1\\)-entries of \\(A\\).  \n\nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nInequality\u202f(1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while construction\u202f(2) exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
