en the rightmost cell of row \\(k\\) is \\((k,\\sigma(k)-1)\\), whose column equals \\(\\sigma(k)-1\\).  \n   By (1) the rectangle’s rightmost column \\(d\\) is at most \\(\\sigma(i)-1<\\sigma(k)-1\\); consequently \\((k,\\sigma(k)-1)\\) cannot belong to the rectangle.  \n   Thus a rectangle cannot contain the rightmost cells of two different rows. ∎  \n\n   Because there are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\ell_i\\ge1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover \\(L\\). By symmetry the same bound holds for \\(R\\). Hence any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(n-1)\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - *Left region* \\(L\\) becomes the strict lower triangle \\(\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\).  \n     For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j=\\{(i,j):j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  \n     The \\(V_j\\) are disjoint and together cover all of \\(L\\); there are \\(n-1\\) of them.  \n   - *Right region* \\(R\\) becomes the strict upper triangle \\(\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\).  \n     For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     of size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\).  \n     The \\(H_i\\) are disjoint, cover all of \\(R\\), and there are \\(n-1\\) of them.\n\n   Altogether we use exactly \\(2\\,(n-1)\\) tiles, meeting the lower bound (2).  \n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2\\,(n-1)\\). For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(2025-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n   \\]\n\nThus the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe board consists of the unit squares \\([i,i+1]\\times[j,j+1]\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  \nDenote by \\(U\\) the set of uncovered squares.  \nBecause each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square, there exists a permutation\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA **tile** is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose vertices lie on grid lines; it consists of a set of\nconsecutive rows and a set of consecutive columns.  \nTiles are required to be pairwise disjoint and none of them may contain a point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Every tile lies completely on one side of all holes in its rows  \n\nLet a tile \\(T\\) occupy the rows \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and the columns \\(c\\le j\\le d\\).  \nFor any row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) the uncovered square of that row is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) cannot contain this square, either \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\) (the whole tile lies left of the hole) or\n\\(c>\\sigma(i)\\) (the whole tile lies right of the hole).  \n\nIf there were rows \\(i,i'\\in[a,b]\\) with \\(d\\ge\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(c\\le\\sigma(i')\\), then the cell\n\\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) or \\((i',\\sigma(i'))\\) would belong to \\(T\\), a contradiction.  \nHence **all rows of a tile satisfy the same inequality**, and consequently every tile is contained either in  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<\\sigma(i)\\}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j):j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the tiling of the whole board splits into a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\); the total\nnumber of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n|{\\cal T}_L|+|{\\cal T}_R|.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Structure of the left region  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) put  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\quad(\\text{the length of the left interval in row }i).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nConsequently