e n-1.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for the whole board  \n\nFrom (5), (8) and (9) we obtain the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\n\\text{any admissible tiling uses at least }2\\,(n-1)\\text{ tiles}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal\n\\((i,i)\\)).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Tilings of \\(L\\).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j):j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are disjoint, avoid the diagonal, and together cover all of \\(L\\).\nHence \\(|{\\cal T}_L|=n-1\\).\n\n*Tilings of \\(R\\).*  \nFor each row \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nof size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are disjoint, avoid the diagonal, and together cover all of \\(R\\).\nThus \\(|{\\cal T}_R|=n-1\\).\n\nThe total number of tiles in this construction is  \n\n\\[\n|{\\cal T}_L|+|{\\cal T}_R|=2\\,(n-1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (10) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2\\,(n-1)\\) tiles, while construction (11)\nshows that exactly \\(2\\,(n-1)\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2\\,(n-1)\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\,(2025-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
