"**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   The condition \u201cexactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to be the cells \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) of a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (here \\(n=2025\\)).  \n\n2. **Separation into two regions.**  \n   Any axis\u2011parallel rectangle cannot contain a hole, hence each rectangle lies entirely either to the left of all holes in its rows or to the right of them.  \n   Consequently the board splits into the **left region**  \n\n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,j):j<\\sigma(i)\\}\n   \\]\n\n   and the **right region**  \n\n   \\[\n   R=\\{(i,j):j>\\sigma(i)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and the tiling of the whole board is the disjoint union of a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).\n\n3. **Lower bound for one region.**  \n   In \\(L\\) the length of row \\(i\\) equals \\(\\ell_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\).  \n   Because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); thus \\(L\\) is a Ferrers diagram having exactly one row of each length \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n\n   *Lemma.* In any partition of such a diagram into axis\u2011parallel rectangles, the rightmost cell of each non\u2011empty row must belong to a different rectangle.  \n\n   *Proof of Lemma.*  \n   Let a rectangle \\(Q\\) occupy rows \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and columns \\(c\\le j\\le d\\).  \n   For every row \\(i\\) in \\([a,b]\\) the rectangle must stay left of the hole in that row, i.e. \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   d\\le \\min_{a\\le i\\le b}\\bigl(\\sigma(i)-1\\bigr).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Suppose two distinct rows \\(i<k\\) share the same rectangle.  \n   Since the row lengths are distinct, \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq\\sigma(k)\\); assume \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(k)\\).  \n   Then the rightmost cell of row \\(k\\) is \\((k,\\sigma(k)-1)\\), whose column equals \\(\\sigma(k)-1\\).  \n   By (1) the rectangle\u2019s rightmost column \\(d\\) is at most \\(\\sigma(i)-1<\\sigma(k)-1\\); consequently \\((k,\\sigma(k)-1)\\) cannot belong to the rectangle.  \n   Thus a rectangle cannot contain the rightmost cells of two different rows. \u220e  \n\n   Because there are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\ell_i\\ge1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover \\(L\\). By symmetry the same bound holds for \\(R\\). Hence any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(n-1)\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - *Left region* \\(L\\) becomes the strict lower triangle \\(\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\).  \n     For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j=\\{(i,j):j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  \n     The \\(V_j\\) are disjoint and together cover all of \\(L\\); there are \\(n-1\\) of them.  \n   - *Right region* \\(R\\) becomes the strict upper triangle \\(\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\).  \n     For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     of size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\).  \n     The \\(H_i\\) are disjoint, cover all of \\(R\\), and there are \\(n-1\\) of them.\n\n   Altogether we use exactly \\(2\\,(n-1)\\) tiles, meeting the lower bound (2).  \n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2\\,(n-1)\\). For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(2025-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n   \\]\n\nThus the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe board consists of the unit squares \\([i,i+1]\\times[j,j+1]\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  \nDenote by \\(U\\) the set of uncovered squares.  \nBecause each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square, there exists a permutation\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA **tile** is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose vertices lie on grid lines; it consists of a set of\nconsecutive rows and a set of consecutive columns.  \nTiles are required to be pairwise disjoint and none of them may contain a point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Every tile lies completely on one side of all holes in its rows  \n\nLet a tile \\(T\\) occupy the rows \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and the columns \\(c\\le j\\le d\\).  \nFor any row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) the uncovered square of that row is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) cannot contain this square, either \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\) (the whole tile lies left of the hole) or\n\\(c>\\sigma(i)\\) (the whole tile lies right of the hole).  \n\nIf there were rows \\(i,i'\\in[a,b]\\) with \\(d\\ge\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(c\\le\\sigma(i')\\), then the cell\n\\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) or \\((i',\\sigma(i'))\\) would belong to \\(T\\), a contradiction.  \nHence **all rows of a tile satisfy the same inequality**, and consequently every tile is contained either in  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<\\sigma(i)\\}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j):j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the tiling of the whole board splits into a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\); the total\nnumber of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n|{\\cal T}_L|+|{\\cal T}_R|.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Structure of the left region  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) put  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\quad(\\text{the length of the left interval in row }i).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nConsequently \\(L\\) is a Ferrers diagram (a left\u2011justified Young diagram) having **exactly one row of each\nlength** \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\); the rows may appear in any order.\n\nFor a row with \\(\\ell_i\\ge1\\) denote its rightmost cell by  \n\n\\[\np_i=(i,\\ell_i).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma: distinct rightmost cells need distinct rectangles  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\text{In any partition of }L\\text{ into axis\u2011parallel rectangles,\nthe cells }p_i\\text{ belonging to different rows lie in different rectangles.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a rectangle \\(Q\\) occupy rows \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and columns \\(c\\le j\\le d\\).  \nBecause \\(Q\\subseteq L\\), for each row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) we must have \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\); otherwise the hole\n\\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) would belong to \\(Q\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nd\\le\\min_{a\\le i\\le b}\\bigl(\\sigma(i)-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAssume that \\(Q\\) contains the rightmost cells of two distinct rows \\(i<k\\).  \nSince the row lengths are distinct, \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq\\sigma(k)\\); without loss of generality\n\\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(k)\\).  \nThen \\(\\sigma(i)-1<\\sigma(k)-1\\).  \nFrom (7) we obtain \\(d\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\), which is strictly smaller than the column\n\\(\\sigma(k)-1\\) of the rightmost cell of row \\(k\\).  \nThus that cell cannot belong to \\(Q\\), a contradiction. \u220e  \n\nBecause there are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\ell_i\\ge1\\), the lemma forces  \n\n\\[\n|{\\cal T}_L|\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The same bound for the right region  \n\nThe right region \\(R\\) consists of the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>\\sigma(i)\\).  \nIts row lengths are \\(r_i=n-\\sigma(i)\\); the multiset \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) is again \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\nRepeating the argument of Lemma\u202f1 with the \u201cleftmost\u2019\u2019 cell of each non\u2011empty row of \\(R\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n|{\\cal T}_R|\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for the whole board  \n\nFrom (5), (8) and (9) we obtain the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\n\\text{any admissible tiling uses at least }2\\,(n-1)\\text{ tiles}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal\n\\((i,i)\\)).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Tilings of \\(L\\).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j):j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are disjoint, avoid the diagonal, and together cover all of \\(L\\).\nHence \\(|{\\cal T}_L|=n-1\\).\n\n*Tilings of \\(R\\).*  \nFor each row \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nof size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are disjoint, avoid the diagonal, and together cover all of \\(R\\).\nThus \\(|{\\cal T}_R|=n-1\\).\n\nThe total number of tiles in this construction is  \n\n\\[\n|{\\cal T}_L|+|{\\cal T}_R|=2\\,(n-1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (10) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2\\,(n-1)\\) tiles, while construction (11)\nshows that exactly \\(2\\,(n-1)\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2\\,(n-1)\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\,(2025-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
