L\\) is a Ferrers diagram (a left‑justified Young diagram) having **exactly one row of each\nlength** \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\); the rows may appear in any order.\n\nFor a row with \\(\\ell_i\\ge1\\) denote its rightmost cell by  \n\n\\[\np_i=(i,\\ell_i).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma: distinct rightmost cells need distinct rectangles  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\text{In any partition of }L\\text{ into axis‑parallel rectangles,\nthe cells }p_i\\text{ belonging to different rows lie in different rectangles.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a rectangle \\(Q\\) occupy rows \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and columns \\(c\\le j\\le d\\).  \nBecause \\(Q\\subseteq L\\), for each row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) we must have \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\); otherwise the hole\n\\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) would belong to \\(Q\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nd\\le\\min_{a\\le i\\le b}\\bigl(\\sigma(i)-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAssume that \\(Q\\) contains the rightmost cells of two distinct rows \\(i<k\\).  \nSince the row lengths are distinct, \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq\\sigma(k)\\); without loss of generality\n\\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(k)\\).  \nThen \\(\\sigma(i)-1<\\sigma(k)-1\\).  \nFrom (7) we obtain \\(d\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\), which is strictly smaller than the column\n\\(\\sigma(k)-1\\) of the rightmost cell of row \\(k\\).  \nThus that cell cannot belong to \\(Q\\), a contradiction. ∎  \n\nBecause there are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\ell_i\\ge1\\), the lemma forces  \n\n\\[\n|{\\cal T}_L|\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The same bound for the right region  \n\nThe right region \\(R\\) consists of the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>\\sigma(i)\\).  \nIts row lengths are \\(r_i=n-\\sigma(i)\\); the multiset \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) is again \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\nRepeating the argument of Lemma 1 with the “leftmost’’ cell of each non‑empty row of \\(R\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n|{\\cal T}_R|\\ge n-