lumns.  \nIf a rectangle contained a cell of \\(A\\) and a cell of \\(B\\), then it would contain the cell \\((\\tau(j),j)\\) for some column \\(j\\), i.e. a hole, which is forbidden.  \nHence **every tile lies entirely in \\(A\\) or entirely in \\(B\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Row‑lengths of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nFix a row \\(i\\).  \nExactly \\(i\\) holes lie in rows \\(1,\\dots ,i\\); therefore the remaining \\(n-i\\) holes lie in rows \\(>i\\).  \nConsequently there are exactly \\(n-i\\) columns \\(j\\) with \\(\\tau(j)>i\\), i.e. with \\((i,j)\\in A\\).  \nThus the number of cells of \\(A\\) in row \\(i\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n|A\\cap\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}|=n-i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nHence the rows of \\(A\\) have lengths  \n\n\\[\nn-1,n-2,\\dots ,1,0,\n\\]\nall distinct for rows \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\nA completely symmetric argument shows that row \\(i\\) of \\(B\\) contains exactly \\(i-1\\) cells; thus the row‑lengths of \\(B\\) are  \n\n\\[\n0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1,\n\\]\nagain all distinct for rows \\(2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the number of rectangles  \n\nConsider the set \\(A\\).  View its cells as a partially ordered set with the product order  \n\\[\n(i,j)\\le (i',j')\\iff i\\le i'\\text{ and }j\\le j'.\n\\]\nA rectangle \\(I\\times J\\) is precisely a **chain** in this poset (all its elements are comparable).  \n\nA classic result of Dilworth (or a direct argument) states that any partition of a finite poset into chains contains at least as many chains as the size of a largest antichain.  \n\nIn \\(A\\) the cells  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{C}=\\{(i,c_i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(c_i\\) is the smallest column index of a cell of \\(A\\) in row \\(i\\), form an antichain: for \\(i<j\\) we have \\(c_i>c_j\\) because the row‑lengths strictly decrease.  Hence \\(|\\mathcal{C}|=n-1\\).  \n\nTherefore any chain decomposition of \\(A\\) (i.e. any rectangle partition) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \nThe same reasoning applied to \\(B\\) yields the identical lower bound \\(n-1\\).  \n\nConsequently **any tiling satisfying the problem’s condition uses at least**\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n**tiles.**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered cells are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n*Upper region \\(A\\).*  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_k^{+}= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R_k^{+}\\) is a \\(k\\times (n-k)\\) rectangle.  For any \\((i,j)\\in R_k^{+}\\) we have \\(i\\le k<j\\), so \\(i<j\\) and \\((i,j)\\) is not a diagonal cell; thus \\(R_k^{+}\\subseteq A\\).  \nThe sets \\(R_1^{+},R_2^{+},\\dots ,R_{n-1}^{+}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union is exactly \\(A\\) (every cell with \\(i<j\\) belongs to the unique rectangle with \\(k=i\\)).  \n\n*Lower region \\(B\\).*  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_k^{-}= \\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R_k^{-}\\) is a \\((n-k)\\times k\\) rectangle.  For any \\((i,j)\\in R_k^{-}\\) we have \\(i>k\\ge j\\), so \\(i>j\\) and \\((i,j)\\) is not a diagonal cell; thus \\(R_k^{-}\\subseteq B\\).  \nAgain the rectangles \\(R_1^{-},\\dots ,R_{n-1}^{-}\\) are disjoint and together cover all of \\(B\\).\n\nAltogether we have used exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangular tiles, and every non‑hole cell is covered exactly once.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction achieving it coincide.  \nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
