se the identity permutation \\(\\tau(j)=j\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a tile covering the whole row segment  \n     \\[\n     R_i^{+}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n     \\]  \n     a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.  These \\(n-1\\) tiles cover all cells of \\(A\\) (the region above the diagonal) and are pairwise disjoint because they lie in different rows.\n\n   - For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a tile covering the whole column segment  \n     \\[\n     R_j^{-}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n     \\]  \n     an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle.  These \\(n-1\\) tiles cover all cells of \\(B\\) (the region below the diagonal) and are pairwise disjoint because they lie in different columns.\n\n   The two families are disjoint from each other (one family lies strictly above the diagonal, the other strictly below), and together they cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) diagonal holes.  The total number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number of tiles, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Number the rows \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square, there is a function  \n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n\\text{the uncovered square in row }i\\text{ is }(i,\\sigma(i)).\n\\]  \nThe same condition for the columns forces \\(\\sigma\\) to be a bijection; hence \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.  \nDefine its inverse \\(\\tau=\\sigma^{-1}\\); thus \\(\\tau(j)\\) is the row of the uncovered square in column \\(j\\).\n\nDenote the set of holes by  \n\\[\nU=\\{(\\tau(j),j)\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Decomposition of the board  \n\nFor a cell \\((i,j)\\) that is not a hole we have either \\(i<\\tau(j)\\) (the cell lies **above** the hole in column \\(j\\)) or \\(i>\\tau(j)\\) (the cell lies **below** that hole).  Define\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<\\tau(j)\\},\\qquad   \nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>\\tau(j)\\}.\n\\]\nThus the board is the disjoint union \\(U\\cup A\\cup B\\).\n\nA rectangular tile is a set of cells of the form \\(I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of rows and columns, respectively.  Suppose a tile \\(T=I\\times J\\) contains a cell of \\(A\\) and a cell of \\(B\\).  Let \\((i_1,j_1)\\in A\\) and \\((i_2,j_2)\\in B\\).  Then \\(i_1<\\tau(j_1)\\) and \\(i_2>\\tau(j_2)\\).  Since \\(I\\) is an interval, it contains every integer between \\(i_1\\) and \\(i_2\\); similarly \\(J\\) contains every integer between \\(j_1\\) and \\(j_2\\).  Consequently \\(T\\) contains the cell \\((\\tau(j_1),j_1)\\) if \\(j_1=j_2\\), or it contains \\((\\tau(j_1),j_2)\\) or \\((i_2,\\tau(j_2))\\) otherwise \u2013 in any case a hole from \\(U\\).  This contradicts the requirement that holes are uncovered.  Hence **every tile lies entirely in \\(A\\) or entirely in \\(B\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the number of tiles in \\(A\\)\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(\\tau(j)>1\\) define the cell\n\\[\na_j=(\\tau(j)-1,\\;j).\n\\]\nThe cell \\(a_j\\) belongs to \\(A\\) and is the unique cell of \\(A\\) that is directly above the hole in column \\(j\\).\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{A rectangle }R\\subseteq A\\text{ can contain at most one cell }a_j.\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nAssume that a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\subseteq A\\) contains two distinct cells \\(a_{j_1}\\) and \\(a_{j_2}\\) with \\(\\tau(j_1)<\\tau(j_2)\\).  Then the rows \\(\\tau(j_1)-1\\) and \\(\\tau(j_2)-1\\) belong to the interval \\(I\\); consequently \\(I\\) also contains the row \\(\\tau(j_2)-1\\).  Since \\(j_1\\in J\\), the cell \\((\\tau(j_2)-1,j_1)\\) lies in \\(R\\).  However column \\(j_1\\) contains cells of \\(A\\) only up to row \\(\\tau(j_1)-1<\\tau(j_2)-1\\); thus \\((\\tau(j_2)-1,j_1)\\notin A\\), contradicting \\(R\\subseteq A\\).  \u220e\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) columns with \\(\\tau(j)>1\\); consequently there are \\(n-1\\) distinct cells \\(a_j\\).  Because each of these cells must be covered by some tile and a tile can cover at most one of them, **at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover \\(A\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the number of tiles in \\(B\\)\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(\\tau(j)<n\\) define the cell\n\\[\nb_j=(\\tau(j)+1,\\;j).\n\\]\nThe cell \\(b_j\\) belongs to \\(B\\) and is the unique cell of \\(B\\) that is directly below the hole in column \\(j\\).\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{A rectangle }R\\subseteq B\\text{ can contain at most one cell }b_j.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\(R=I\\times J\\subseteq B\\) contains \\(b_{j_1}\\) and \\(b_{j_2}\\) with \\(\\tau(j_1)<\\tau(j_2)\\).  Then the rows \\(\\tau(j_1)+1\\) and \\(\\tau(j_2)+1\\) belong to \\(I\\); hence \\(I\\) also contains the row \\(\\tau(j_1)+1\\).  Since \\(j_2\\in J\\), the cell \\((\\tau(j_1)+1,j_2)\\) lies in \\(R\\).  But column \\(j_2\\) contains cells of \\(B\\) only for rows larger than \\(\\tau(j_2)\\ge \\tau(j_1)+1\\); therefore \\((\\tau(j_1)+1,j_2)\\notin B\\), a contradiction.  \u220e\n\nExactly one column has \\(\\tau(j)=n\\); therefore there are \\(n-1\\) cells \\(b_j\\).  As before, each must be covered and a rectangle can contain at most one, so **at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover \\(B\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Overall lower bound  \n\nSince every admissible tiling consists of tiles lying in \\(A\\) together with tiles lying in \\(B\\), the total number of tiles is at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this gives the bound \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\tau(j)=j\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Tiles covering the region \\(A\\) (above the diagonal).*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle\n\\[\nR_i^{+}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(R_i^{+}\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle lying entirely in \\(A\\).  The rectangles \\(R_1^{+},\\dots ,R_{n-1}^{+}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy different rows, and their union is exactly \\(A\\).\n\n*Tiles covering the region \\(B\\) (below the diagonal).*  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle\n\\[\nR_j^{-}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(R_j^{-}\\) is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle lying entirely in \\(B\\).  The rectangles \\(R_1^{-},\\dots ,R_{n-1}^{-}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy different columns, and their union is exactly \\(B\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint from each other (one lies strictly above, the other strictly below the diagonal), and together they cover every unit square except the diagonal holes.  The total number of tiles used is\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit tiling achieving this bound coincide.  Hence the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
